hear	V;NFIN	hear	We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 22-506, Biden versus Nebraska.
please	V;NFIN	please	Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: COVID-19 is the most devastating pandemic in our nation's history and it has caused enormous disruption and economic distress.
struggle	V;PST;V.PTCP	struggled	Over the past three years, millions of Americans have struggled to pay rent, utilities, food, and many have been unable to pay their debts.
head	V;NFIN	head	To head off immediate harm for student-loan borrowers, two secrataries across two administrations invoked the HEROES Act to suspend interest and payment obligations for all Americans with federally held loans.
end	V;PRS;SG;3	ends	But, if that forbearance ends without further relief, it's undisputed that defaults and delinquencies will surge above pre-pandemic levels.
invoke	V;PST;SG;3	invoked	So Secretary Cardona again invoked the HEROES Act to provide a measure of loan forgiveness to ensure that this unprecedented pandemic does not leave borrowers worse off in relation to their student loans.
ask	V;PRS;PL;3	ask	The states ask this Court to deny that vital relief to millions of Americans, but they lack standing to seek that result.
assert	V;PRS;PL;3	assert	They principally assert harm to a separate legal person, MOHELA, that could sue in its own name but has chosen not to do so, and the states' asserted harms to their tax revenues are self-inflicted and indirect.
demand	V;PRS;SG;3	demands	The states' bare disagreement with this policy is not the sort of concrete injury that Article III demands.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	On the merits, the states say the Act doesn't authorize the Secretary to ever forgive loan principal.
authorize	V;PST;SG;3	authorized	Congress expressly authorized the Secretary to waive or modify any Title IV provision in emergencies to provide financial relief to borrowers.
act	V;PST;SG;3	acted	Loan forgiveness is a paradigmatic form of debt relief, and the Secretary acted within the heartland of his authority and in line with the central purpose of the HEROES Act in providing that relief here.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	To apply the major questions doctrine to override that clear text would deny borrowers critical relief that Congress authorized and the Secretary deemed essential.
welcome	V;PRS;SG;1	welcome	I welcome the Court's questions.
appear	V;PRS;SG;3	appears	This appears at JA 261.
sign	V;PST;SG;3	signed	That was the decision document that the Secretary signed where he said, I hereby issue waivers and modifications of multiple provisions under Title IV of the student loan program.
repeat	V;PST;V.PTCP	repeated	And then that language was repeated in the Federal Register notice that actually implemented that program and constitutes the final agency action that the states are challenging here.
explain	V;NFIN	explain	Well, could you explain then -- in -- in -- in other provisions, there is express language as to cancellation, and, of course, there isn't here.
take	V;NFIN	take	So would you take a minute to explain how a waiver or modification amounts to a waiver -- to a cancellation?
identify	V;PST;SG;3	identified	So the Secretary identified various provisions in Title IV that govern the terms and conditions of student loans and also govern discharge and cancellation in other circumstances, as your question suggested.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	And I think the straightforward way to think about how the verbs map onto the Secretary's action is that he waived elements of those provisions that contain eligibility requirements for discharge and cancellation that are inapplicable under this program and then modified the provisions to contain the limitations that he had announced as part and parcel of announcing this loan forgiveness.
suggest	V;PST;V.PTCP	suggested	Now you had suggested that there's no express statement in the HEROES Act to discharge loan principal, and that's true, but the relevant and operative language here is the provision that says the Secretary is empowered to waive or modify any Title IV provision, and so the HEROES Act isn't enumerating any of the various forms of relief that have long been authorized and implemented under this statute.
think	V;NFIN	think	I don't think anything can be read into the fact that there's no express reference to particular forms of relief because Congress was trying to broadly cover the field and ensure that the Secretary had the tools to respond to the national emergency with whatever relief might be necessitated.
have	V;PST;PL;1	had	But, in an opinion we had a few years ago by Justice Scalia, he talked about what the word "modify" means, and he said modified in our view connotes moderate change.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	He said it might be good English to say that the French Revolution modified the status of the French nobility, but only because there's a figure of speech called understatement and a literary device known as sarcasm.
talk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	talking	We're talking about half a trillion dollars and 43 million Americans.
fit	V;NFIN	fit	How does that fit under the normal understanding of "modifying"?
recognize	V;PRS;SG;1	recognize	So, of course, I recognize that in MCI, Justice Scalia's opinion adopted a narrower understanding of that term, but I don't read that opinion to set forth a universal meaning of "modify, "no matter the statutory context.
have	V;PRS;PL;1	have	And, here, of course, we have a broader phrase, "waive or modify."
contest	V;PRS;V.PTCP	contesting	It's undisputed and the states aren't contesting that the ordinary meaning of "waive" means to eliminate an obligation in its entirety.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	And I think, if you look at that phrase in the context of the statute, that means that "modify" has to mean making a change up to the point of wholesale elimination.
say	V;NFIN	say	It would be really strange for Congress to say you can eliminate obligations altogether or tweak them just the littlest bit, but you can't do anything in between.
waive	V;NFIN	waive	Well, but it's "waive "particular regulatory or statutory provisions.
suggest	V;PRS;SG;3	suggests	That to me suggests a much more focused use of the word.
waive	V;NFIN	waive	Well, it's "waive or modify" paired with the authority to do that with respect to any Title IV provision.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	So I think that that is the –
say	V;NFIN	say	It doesn't say waive -- modify or waive loan balances.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
give	V;NFIN	give	To give you a sense of how we're dividing our argument, I will be addressing the issue of waiver of tort, the new cause of action, we say, of disgorgement for wrongdoing.
address	V;NFIN	address	My colleague, Mr. Eizenga, will address the Criminal Code issue and unjust enrichment.
act	V;PRS;SG;3	acts	And Mr. Kelly, who acts for the remaining appellants, will address the requirement that any order of disgorgement of profits must be limited to those profits that result from, or that are causally linked to the breach of contract or other wrongdoing at issue.
agree	V;PST;V.PTCP	agreed	And we have agreed to divide our time 20 20 20.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	And I wish to start by clarifying a point of terminology, and it relates to the use of the term waiver of tort.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	And the reason I wish to clarify it is that you will hear the respondent's counsel say waiver of tort is nothing new, that it's an old cause of action.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	And therefore what the Newfoundland and Labrador Court of Appeal did is -- is nothing out of the ordinary.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	But what I say that argument obscures is the fact that waiver of tort can be used in two quite different ways, and t- to refer to two different concepts.
refer	V;NFIN	refer	The first is, it may refer to an old, old common law concept, a construct that would allow a plaintiff to obtain a gains based remedy but for a completed cause of action, and traditionally it was uh applied with respect to two causes of action, in particular proprietary causes of action and trespass or conversion.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	But what I wish to emphasize is that that old construct was applied where the plaintiff could prove a completed cause of action.
remain	V;PRS;SG;3	remains	It remains an open question whether that construct should be expanded to allow for gains-based remedies for other types of claims.
plead	V;NFIN	plead	This is this is your case to plead, but you have dived headlong into the weeds.
tell	V;PST;V.PTCP	told	You have not told us what our task is here today.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	Now that's up to you, whether you want to frame it in a more general sense or not.
assist	V;NFIN	assist	But it would assist me, at least in terms of what it is, in a general sense, we're being asked to decide today.
ask	V;PRS;V.PTCP	asking	So what I am asking you to decide today is the second -- relates to the second, and what I say is the new and much narrower meaning of of waiver of tort, which is what the Newfoundland and Labrador Court of Appeal did, which is to say there exists a cause of action that comprises a breach of a duty of care without causally linked loss or injury.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	And that, I say, is very new.
suggest	V;PST;V.PTCP	suggested	It was first suggested by the Ontario courts back in Sirhan, and it's the Newfoundland and Labrador Court of Appeal that, for the first time has affirmatively recognized that as a cause of action.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	And what I would ask this court to do is to clarify, to correct that error and say that there is no such cause of action.
take	V;PRS;SG;1	take	I take it this is a motion to strike.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	That's what I mean by general propositions.
arise	V;PST;SG;3	arose	It arose at first instance on a motion to strike.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	And so I have -- there are really two main reasons I say why the proposed cause of action should be definitively ruled by this court, not to be sound at law.
lack	V;PRS;SG;3	lacks	And the second is that even on its own terms, it lacks internal coherence and is impermissibly vague, I say, and unworkable.
oppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	opposed	And for those reasons, the commentators have opposed it.
put	V;NFIN	put	It would put -- no other common law jurisdiction has even considered such a cause of action.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	And fundamentally, I say it doesn't answer any unmet societal need.
turn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	turning	So turning to the first point, which is the the incompatibility with the law of negligence, that law, of course, has been settled for close to 100 years, and it couldn't be clearer that breach of a duty of care without loss or injury does not give rise to a cause of action.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	set	That's set out in Moran and Pyle.
need	V;PST;V.PTCP	needed	If needed, that's in my condensed book at Tab 3.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	And what I wish to emphasize is that this court has made it abundantly clear that careless behavior alone is not wrongful.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	It is only the loss or injury that makes it wrongful.
direct	V;NFIN	direct	And if I could direct the court's attention, please, to Tab 4 of our condensed book.
read	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Reading	Reading from line E a quote from Lord Diplock and Browning and War Office:
act	V;PRS;SG;3	acts	"A person who acts without reasonable care does no wrong in law.
commit	V;PRS;SG;3	commits	He commits no tort.
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	He only does wrong, he only commits a tort, if his lack of care causes damage to the plaintiff."
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	And the same point is made in Clements and Clements over at Tab 8, please.
cause	V;PRS;SG;3	causes	He is a wrongdoer only in respect of the damage which he actually causes to the plaintiff."
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	And this point is particularly important because the proposed cause of action is said to be disgorgement for wrongdoing.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	And I say the court below proceeded on the basis that carelessness, failure to meet the standard of care, was wrongdoing.
tell	V;PST;V.PTCP	told	But that is is exactly the opposite to what the law of negligence has told us.
feel	V;NFIN	Feel	To Feel Happier, We Have to Resolve to the Life We Evolved to Live
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	When humans had to hunt for food, they had to move more.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Sedentary lifestyles have made humans far less active than they need to be.
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	“Hunting Wooly Mammoth” by http://cloudinary.com is licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
have	V;PRS;PL;1	have	When we have to give a talk to a group of people, we feel anxious and experience the bodily fear responses that do not make sense now: The system is not meant to function in this safe context.
tell	V;PRS;SG;1	tell	As a psychiatrist specialized in anxiety and trauma, I often tell my patients and students that to understand how fear works in us, we have to see it in the context where it evolved.
frown	V;PST;SG;3	frowned	Ten thousand years ago, if another human frowned at us, chances were high one of us would be dead in a couple minutes.
like	V;NFIN	like	In the tribal life of our ancestors, if other tribe members did not like you, you would be dead, or exiled and dead.
evolve	V;PRS;PL;3	evolve	Biological evolution is very slow, but civilization, culture, society and technology evolve relatively fast.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	It takes around a million years for evolutionary change to happen in a species, and people have been around for about 200,000 years.
see	V;PRS;SG;3	sees	Each of us, however, sees drastic changes in our lifestyle and environment over a matter of a few years.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s compare the life for which this body and brain has evolved with the life we live, from my perspective as a scientist and a physician.
eat	V;PRS;PL;1	eat	What we eat
evolve	V;PST;PL;1	evolved	We evolved in a context of scarcity, and had to walk or run for miles and scavenge to find food; we had to work for it.
eat	V;PST;PL;1	ate	When we ate, we did not know when the next time would be.
have	V;PST;PL;1	had	When we had it, we were better off eating the high-calorie food that increased our chance of survival when hungry (greasy food feels appealing).
digest	V;NFIN	digest	Food was not quick to digest, stomachs also had to work hard for it: meat, fruits, vegetables, seeds and nuts.
contain	V;PST;SG;3	contained	None of these contained “easy” sugary calories.
evolve	V;PST;SG;3	evolved	The body that was prone to starvation, evolved to eat it all when available, and store it, and be stingy in its use.
consume	V;NFIN	consume	But now, food is just a few feet away, and we can easily consume thousands of calories in one serving.
require	V;PST;SG;3	required	Fatty food, which required days and miles of hunting and scavenging to acquire, is now right there in the fridge or at the McDonald's.
give	V;PRS;V.PTCP	giving	It is like giving full fridge privileges to your Labrador.
provide	V;PRS;PL;3	provide	Easy calories like soda and candies provide a very large amount of energy in a very short amount of time and confuse the whole system.
end	V;PRS;PL;1	end	We end up fat and fatigued.
move	V;PRS;PL;1	move	How we move
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	Our ancestors had to be active to survive in the wild.
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	They had to walk miles a day, and were frequently involved in high intensity physical activity: moving heavy objects, climbing, fighting, chasing a prey or fleeing predators.
evolve	V;NFIN	evolve	This body did not evolve to sit at a desk eight hours a day, and then lie on a couch for the rest of the day munching on high calorie food, looking at a small or large screen.
walk	V;NFIN	walk	A contemporary American may only walk a few dozen steps to the car, drive to work, ride the elevator to the office, walk a few dozen steps back to the car, get drive-thru food, then be back on the couch.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	Suboptimal muscle use leads to weaker joint support, and weird postures cause pain.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	Pain leads to reduced activity, more obesity and weaker muscles; then comes opioid epidemic, overuse of pain medications, and back surgeries for all! [...]
do	V;NFIN	do	What can we do to feel better?
adopt	V;PRS;PL;1	adopt	When we adopt a pet, we learn about their normal environment, activity level and nutrition.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	Isn’t it interesting that we do not apply that to the animal we live in?
feel	V;NFIN	feel	To feel normal, we should live normal, and a human’s normal life is that for which she or he has evolved.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	So I believe the first step is to understand ourselves, and why we do what we do, and desire what we desire.
crave	V;PRS;PL;1	crave	When we crave fatty food, or cannot stop eating, that is because the human animal had to do so to survive.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	Such understanding brings empathy, reduces judgment and helps us get creative.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep away the high-calorie sugary food.
tell	V;PRS;SG;1	tell	I tell my patients: do not buy it, or if you do, buy in small amounts.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try to eat what you were evolved to eat.
know	V;IMP;2	Know	Know the body is lazy, because it wants to save precious energy.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	The drag of going to the gym may be because of that.
know	V;IMP;2	know	Also know that this body would be much happier when it is regularly, and highly active.
know	V;PRS;PL;1	know	We know that exercise is not only helpful for cardiovascular and bodily health, but also reduces anxiety.
ask	V;PRS;SG;1	ask	I ask all my patients to commit to some level of exercise, as part of their treatment plan.
have	V;NFIN	have	And it does not have to be treadmill or gym.
rock	V;PRS;SG;3	rocks	Whatever rocks your boat: yoga, boxing, walking, running up the stairs at work, doing 20 push-ups, 20 sit-ups and 20 squats a day, or dancing to a TV ad; whatever makes your heart pound faster.
come	V;PRS;PL;3	come	Other bonuses come with exercise: getting sun exposure or to know your neighbor when walking the dog, making new friends (or a date) at the gym, feeling better about yourself, and being more attractive to yourself and others.
lift	V;PRS;PL;3	lift	All these factors lift your mood.
feel	V;IMP;2	feel	Do not feel disappointed if you did not lose weight, it’s not the only goal.
increase	V;PST;V.PTCP	increased	Other benefits are abundant and even more important: increased strength and energy, cardiovascular and joint health, improved mood, etc.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	When it comes to sleep, changing behavior works.
avoid	V;IMP;2	avoid	Here are some tips for better sleep: avoid late caffeine, bright screens (including your phone – Facebook can wait), and use your bed only for sleep and sex.
need	V;NFIN	need	Finally, you may need a regular dose of healthy and safe exposure to real-life excitement, and a little bit of fear.
treat	V;PST;PL;1	treated	Bottom line: If we treated our body the way responsible dog owners treated their dog, we would live a much happier life.
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	"Andrews clutch job" by Bob n Renee is licensed under CC BY 2.0
lose	V;PRS;V.PTCP	losing	My town is losing a car repair shop.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	The gas station at the end of Main Street will continue to sell gas, but the garage attached to it is going to close.
have	V;NFIN	have	The mechanic, who has been there for twenty-five years, will have to find work elsewhere.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He had a loyal following; a friend told me he once opened early on a Sunday to fix her tire.
close	V;PRS;V.PTCP	closing	The garage isn’t closing for lack of customers.
close	V;PRS;V.PTCP	closing	Nor is it closing because of taxes, that ritual demon of the political Right.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	A major reason, the owner said, is technology, and the way manufacturers are making cars so complex.
fix	V;NFIN	fix	To fix them requires sophisticated computers that a small shop can’t afford.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	“There isn’t a car from the ’70s or ’80s that we can’t work on,” the owner told our local weekly.
make	V;NFIN	make	“But we’re not in the position to make the investment in all the electronics and computers.
make	V;NFIN	make	To make that transition would be cost prohibitive.”
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Independent repair shops are going out of business all over the country.
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	In California, more than half the gas stations had repair shops as recently as ten years ago.
force	V;PRS;V.PTCP	forcing	Part of that is because oil companies are forcing out independent owners and taking over the retail end themselves.
notice	V;PST;V.PTCP	noticed	(Have you noticed that prices are rising?)
repair	V;NFIN	repair	But partly too it’s because the local shops can’t repair the new computerized cars.
bear	V;PRS;SG;3	bears	This is a trend that bears more scrutiny than it has received.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	It goes to two of the central narratives of our economy — the conventional version at least.
prevail	V;PRS;PL;3	prevail	One is that virtue and hard work prevail, which in this case is not the case.
succumb	V;PST;V.PTCP	succumbed	Hard work and virtue have succumbed to corporate contrivance.
shock	V;PST;V.PTCP	shocked	You are shocked?
cast	V;PRS;SG;3	casts	The other narrative is the one that casts technology as Savior.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	The word itself has become practically a synonym for “future.”
portray	V;PST;V.PTCP	portrayed	Computers are portrayed as the ultimate instruments of democracy.
put	V;NFIN	put	They will put knowledge — and therefore power — on every desktop.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	They will cause hierarchies and bureaucracies to crumble.
herd	V;PST;SG;3	herded	“While the Industrial Revolution herded people into gigantic social institutions — big corporations, big unions, big governments,” wrote Newt Gingrich in his book To Renew America, “the Information Revolution is breaking up these giants and leading us back to something that is — strangely enough — much more like de Tocqueville’s 1830s America.”
have	V;NFIN	have	Brother Newton is a busy man, and maybe he just didn’t have time to consider the complications.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	For one thing, there’s the matter of agency.
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	As David Noble observed in his book Forces of Production, technology is not an impersonal force.
have	V;NFIN	have	It does not have an inevitable evolutionary path.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	Technology is a projection of those who make it, including corporate interests and proprietary claims.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	This can lead to a lot of trouble.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	Noble looks in particular at the machine tool industry, and how it evolved to enable top-down management control instead of autonomy on the shop floor.
go	V;PST;V.PTCP	gone	The technology could have gone either way; it was the corporate managers who made the call.
follow	V;PST;V.PTCP	followed	Computers have followed a similar pattern.
liberate	V;NFIN	liberate	Potentially they can liberate the desktop, and sometimes do.
do	V;PRS;PL;3	do	But in practice they often do the opposite.
monitor	V;NFIN	monitor	Now the boss can monitor your every keystroke.
plant	V;NFIN	plant	At home corporations can plant little spies on your machine.
track	V;NFIN	track	The Feds can track your personal e-mail exchanges.
think	V;PRS;2;SG	think	You think the Department of Homeland Security might have a computer or two?
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	built	And so with the technology built into cars.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	It would seem possible to design it in a way that made cars simpler and less expensive to repair.
know	V;NFIN	know	I don’t really know, but it does seem reasonable.
fit	V;NFIN	fit	The problem is, would it fit the business plans of the corporations that make the cars?
want	V;NFIN	want	Why would General Motors want to put more capacity and control into the hands of independent garages like the one in my town?
want	V;NFIN	want	Wouldn’t it want to lock a customer into its own repair and dealer network, much the way computer printer manufacturers try to lock us into their expensive cartridges?
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	That seems to be what’s happening.
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	Car repair used to be a knowledge commons, shared in driveways, urban curbsides, and voc. ed. classes.
get	V;NFIN	Get	Get yourself some tools and you were ready to go for most routine repairs.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was little if any secret and proprietary code.
help	V;NFIN	help	Repair manuals could help with the more technical issues, but you’d probably go to a shop for those anyway. [...]
suggest	V;PRS;V.PTCP	suggesting	I am not suggesting that we all go back to bamboo huts, though a few weeks might not be the worst thing now and then.
question	V;PRS;V.PTCP	questioning	I am just questioning the techno-romantics who think technology by its very nature is enlarging and fulfilling.
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	Sometimes it does the opposite, and hollows us out.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Adam Smith actually had a glimmer of this, regarding the effect of the division of labor upon the workers involved.
become	V;PRS;SG;3	becomes	As each task becomes more specialized, Smith noted, it engages less of the person.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	Narrow work leads to human atrophy; it can make people “as stupid and ignorant as it is possible for a human creature to become.”
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	(Smith had a brooding cautionary side that is lacking among his acolytes today.)
happen	V;PRS;V.PTCP	happening	What’s happening now is bigger and more endemic.
affect	V;PRS;SG;3	affects	It affects people not just as employees but as “consumers” even more (and consumption is the real work of our “economy” to begin with.)
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	This is a big topic, obviously, but a lot goes back to who is devising the technology and why.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	There’s a built-in social dimension that engages us at more levels, as producers as well as just consumers.
evolve	V;PRS;PL;3	evolve	Open systems evolve to serve the needs of users rather than of those who seek to use the users for their own ends.
compare	V;IMP;2	Compare	Compare, for example, traditional fairy tales with the kiddy entertainment today that has embedded products such as candy bars and colas.
write	V;PRS;SG;3	writes	When someone finally writes the sequel to The Wealth of Nations, open systems and decentralized technologies will be, I think, a central theme.
Involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	Involved	Perhaps a working title could be From the Wealth of Nations to the Well-Being of the People Involved.
tell	V;PRS;2;SG	tell	It was spring, the barren time in March when you can not be sure if it is really warmer, but you are so desperate for change that you tell yourself the mud at the edge of the sidewalk is different from winter mud and you are sure that the smell of wet soil has suddenly a bit of the scent of summer rains, of grass and drowned earthworms.
stir	V;PRS;V.PTCP	stirring	And it has, because it is spring and inside the ground something is stirring.
wear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wearing	I was wearing a yellow linen dress which my mother had picked out and which I therefore disliked although I knew it flattered me.
concentrate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	concentrating	My shoes were white and I was concentrating on keeping them out of the mud.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	My father and I were going to mass — my mother did not go; she was Protestant.
put	V;PST;SG;3	put	My father put his hand on top of my hair, his palm on my head, and I could feel the bone of my skull and my skin and his hot palm, so dry and strong.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	When I was a little girl, he did that often, and called me Muscles.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	He had not called me Muscles or put his hand on my head for a long time.
help	V;NFIN	help	I could not help arching my back a little, I wanted to push against his hand like a cat but the instinct that comes with being thirteen, the half-understood caution that makes a girl timid, or wild, the shyness told me to just walk.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	I wanted to feel the rough edge of the pocket of his coat against my cheek, but I was too tall.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	I wanted to be seven again, and safe.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	But I still wanted to push against his hand and put my hand in his pocket and steal the leather palmed glove, that secret animal.
go	V;PST;SG;1	went	Instead I went into the church, took a Bulletin, dipped my finger in Holy Water and genuflected.
smell	V;PST;SG;3	smelled	The inside of the church smelled like damp wood and furniture polish, not alive at all.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	My father took off his coat and draped it over the edge of the pew and when I came back from communion I stole his glove.
take	V;PST;SG;1	took	The paper taste of the wafer was still in my mouth and I took a deep breath of the leather.
smell	V;PST;SG;3	smelled	It smelled like March.
walk	V;PST;PL;1	walked	We walked back through the school because it was drizzling, my father tall in his navy suit and my shoes going click on the linoleum.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were two classes of each grade, starting at the sixth and going down to the first.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The hall ended in a T and we went left through the gym, walked underneath the bleachers and stood next to the side door, waiting for the rain to stop.
like	V;PST;SG;3	liked	My father was a young man, thirty-five, younger because he liked to be outside, to play softball on Saturday and to take my mother and me camping on vacation.
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	He stood rocked back on his heels with his coat thrown over his shoulders and his hands in his pockets.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	I thought of bacon and eggs, toast with peach jam out of the jar.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were things in the shadows; a metal pail, a mop, rags.
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	Next to the door was a tall wrought-iron candle holder — the kind that stood at either end of the altar.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was no holder and the end was jagged.
sell	V;PST;V.PTCP	sold	They were sold at eighth-grade basketball games on Friday nights.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	The light from the door made the shadows under the bleachers darker, the long space stretched far away.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	I heard the rain and the faint rustle of paper and smelled damp concrete.
go	V;NFIN	go	I did not go near my father but kept my hand in my pocket, feeling the soft leather glove.
be	V;PST;PL;3	was	There was a rustling on the concrete and the drizzle of soft rain.
wonder	V;PST;SG;1	wondered	I wondered if anyone ever went back under the bleachers, if there were crickets or mice there.
wish	V;PST;SG;1	wished	I wished the rain would stop.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	I wanted to go home.
make	V;PST;SG;1	made	I made noises with my heels but they were too loud so I stopped.
click	V;PST;SG;3	clicked	Something else clicked and I tried to see what it was but couldn’t see anything.
clear	V;PST;SG;3	cleared	My father cleared his throat, looking out the door.
imagine	V;PST;SG;1	imagined	I imagined a man down there in the dark, an escaped convict or a madman.
stop	V;PST;V.PTCP	stopped	It had nearly stopped raining.
fry	V;NFIN	fry	In fifteen minutes we would be home and my mother would fry eggs.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	I heard a noise like paper.
hear	V;PST;SG;3	heard	My father heard it, too, but he pretended not to, at least he didn’t turn his head.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	And there was a heavier sound, a rasp, like a box pulled over concrete.
look	V;PST;SG;1	looked	I looked at my father but he didn’t turn his head.
wish	V;PST;SG;1	wished	I wished he would turn his head.
be	V;PST;PL;3	was	There was a click again and the rustle, and I could not think of what it could be.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	I had no explanation for the particular combination of sounds.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	No doubt there was, some two things that happened to be making noises at the same time.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	Once in a fever I heard thousands of birds outside my window and I was terrified that they would fling themselves through the glass and attack me, but it was only the rain on the eaves.
guide	V;PST;V.PTCP	guided	My father’s actions were largely incomprehensible to me, guided by his own secret and labyrinthine calculations.
dwell	V;PST;SG;3	dwelt	He dwelt in another world, a world of intrigue, bargains, contracts and clandestine purchases of land all over the island.
prompt	V;PST;SG;3	prompted	No doubt his decisions were perfectly logical in his own eyes — even the one that prompted him, a patriotic islander, to bring me a tutor from Bain: Master Lunre, an Olondrian.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	The day began as it usually did when my father was expected home from his travels, the house festooned with flowers and stocked with coconut liquor.
stand	V;PST;PL;1	stood	We stood by the gate, washed and perfumed and arrayed in our brightest clothes, my mother twisting her hands in her skirt, my father’s wife with red eyes.
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	Jom, grown taller and broad in the shoulders, moaned gently to himself, while I stood nervously rubbing the heel of one sandal on the flagstones.
scan	V;PST;PL;1	scanned	We scanned the deep blue valley for the first sign of the company, but before we saw them we heard the children shouting: “A yellow man!”
glance	V;PST;PL;1	glanced	We glanced at one another in confusion.
bite	V;PST;SG;3	bit	My mother bit her lower lip; Jom gave a groan of alarm.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	At first I thought the children meant my father, whose golden skin, the color of the night-monkey’s pelt, was a rarity in the islands; but certainly the children of Tyom were familiar with my father, and would never have greeted a council-member with such ill-mannered yells.
remember	V;PST;SG;1	remembered	Then I remembered the only “yellow man” I had ever seen, an Olondrian wizard and doctor who had visited Tyom in my childhood, who wore two pieces of glass on his eyes, attached to his ears with wires, and roamed the hills of Tinimavet, cutting bits off the trees.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	I have since learned that that doctor wrote a well-received treatise, On the Medicinal Properties of the Juice of the Young Coconut, and died a respected man in his native city of Deinivel; but at the time I felt certain he had returned with his sack of tree-cuttings.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“There they are,” said Pavit, the head house-servant, in a strained voice.
weave	V;PRS;V.PTCP	weaving	And there they were: a chain of riders weaving among the trees.
plait	V;PST;V.PTCP	plaited	My father’s plaited umbrella appeared, his still, imposing figure, and beside him another man, tall and lean, astride an island mule.
precede	V;PST;PL;3	preceded	The hectic screams of the children preceded the company into the village, so that they advanced like a festival, drawing people out of their houses.
approach	V;PST;PL;3	approached	As they approached I saw that my father’s face was shining with pride, and his bearing had in it a new hauteur, like that of the old island kings.
ride	V;PST;SG;3	rode	The man who rode beside him, looking uncomfortable with his long legs, kept his gaze lowered and fixed between the ears of his plodding mule.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He was not yellow, but very pale brown, the color of raw cashews; he had silver hair, worn cropped close to the skull so that it resembled a cap.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	collecting	He was not the leaf-collecting doctor, but an altogether strange man, with silver eyebrows in his smooth face and long fine-knuckled hands.
dismount	V;PST;SG;3	dismounted	As he dismounted in front of the house I heard my mother whispering: “Protect us, God with the Black-and-White Tail, from that which is not of this earth.”
dismount	V;PST;SG;3	dismounted	My father dismounted from his mule and strutted toward us, grinning.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	I thought I caught an odor off him, of fish, sea-sickness and sweat.
kneel	V;PST;PL;1	knelt	We knelt and stared down at the bald ground, murmuring ritual greetings, until he touched the tops of our heads with the palm of his fleshy hand.
stand	V;PST;PL;1	stood	Then we stood, unable to keep from staring at the stranger, who faced us awkwardly, half-smiling, taller than any man there.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews meteorological experts on Cyclone Phalin
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Cyclone Phailin has winds that have been measured at 200 km / h, as it surges over land will it begin to lose strength?
drive	V;PST;V.PTCP	driven	Yes, all tropical cyclones are driven by high heat content waters.
move	V;PRS;SG;3	moves	Once a storm moves over any appreciably sized piece of land, the moisture source is removed and the storm begins to dissipate.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	As of the last advisory, T C Phailin has winds of 190 kilometers per hour and has moved inland, headed on a northwest track.
be	V;NFIN	be	However, there will be very heavy rains and flooding along its path.
lose	V;NFIN	loose	Yes, it will loose strength steadily as more and more of this swirling system moves from being over ocean to being over land.
occur	V;PRS;SG;3	occurs	This occurs for two reasons:
move	V;PRS;SG;3	moves	As it moves over land, it is cut off from the source of energy driving the storm [which is] the evaporation of water from the warm sea surface;
increase	V;PST;V.PTCP	Increased	Increased friction - the ocean surface is much, much smoother than the land surface.
sustain	V;PST;V.PTCP	sustained	Tropical cyclones are sustained by a form of energy called latent heat, which is released by moisture evaporated from the ocean that condenses to form rain.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	As tropical cyclones make landfall, this energy source is cut-off and they rapidly lose strength as they move over land.
lose	V;NFIN	lose	Yes, the Cyclone is almost certain to lose strength as it surges over land.
gather	V;PRS;PL;3	gather	Cyclones gather their strength through scraping moisture and heat from warm ocean water that it is not the case over land.
reduce	V;PRS;SG;3	reduces	In the mean time, much stronger friction over land quickly reduces their strength.
lose	V;PRS;PL;3	lose	All tropical cyclones lose strength once they make landfall.
remain	V;NFIN	remain	However, they can remain dangerous storms due to very heavy rains and subsequent landslides, and river flooding.
weaken	V;NFIN	weaken	Phailin's winds will rapidly weaken as it pushes inland.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	Once Cyclone Phailin comes on shore it will immediately begin to lose strength.
contain	V;NFIN	contain	However, and this is important, it still will contain lots of rainfall making flooding an almost certainty.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	A previous cyclone in 1999 in the Bay of Bengal area of India left 10,000 people dead.
prepare	V;PST;V.PTCP	prepared	Is the Indian government well prepared to deal with this cyclone?
follow	V;PST;V.PTCP	followed	I have not followed that aspect of the societal response for the present storm.
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	However, historically, there have been several events that should cause a societal response.
learn	V;PRS;PL;1	learn	Hopefully, we all learn from past mistakes.
kill	V;PST;V.PTCP	killed	The history of strong cyclones and death in the region is notable, with at least 5 large cyclone events in the past 35 years with 10,000 and as many as 300,000 people killed, millions left homeless and estimated damages as high as US$ 10 billion.
have	V;NFIN	have	I don't have enough information to answer this question, one way or the other.
read	V;PST;V.PTCP	read	From all the press reports that I have read, the Indian government appears to have taken the threat of Cyclone Phailin very seriously indeed.
prepare	V;PRS;V.PTCP	preparing	The government has been much more pro-active in preparing for this cyclone than in the past.
carry	V;PST;V.PTCP	carried	Mandatory mass evacuations have been carried out, which is essential to minimize loss of life in these situations.
occur	V;NFIN	occur	Unfortunately, extensive property damage is bound to occur even with the best preparation.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	I heard that they orchestrated the largest people evacuation to a natural disaster in Indian history.
applaud	V;PST;V.PTCP	applauded	This shall be applauded given the size and strength of Phailin.
visit	V;PST;SG;1	visited	I visited the damaged area of the 1999 storm near Bhubaneshwar in the summer of 2012 for an invited symposium and summer school on tropical cyclones [...] sponsored by the Indian government.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given the living standard and residence of people living near the coastal areas in this region, the evacuation is certainly necessary and essential to save lives.
comment	V;NFIN	comment	I cannot comment directly on how the Indian government was prepared for this cyclone.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	However, the news reports (BBC etc.) were very encouraging in that the authorities were reportedly making strenuous efforts to evacuate the population from near the coast and areas that are prone to flooding.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	From all reports that I have seen, the government was well prepared for this cyclone.
know	V;NFIN	know	For this question I don't know the 'preparedness' of the Indian gov't to deal with this.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews Mario J. Lucero and Isabel Ruiz of Heaven Sent Gaming
form	V;PST;PL;1	formed	And, together we formed this thing, it's sort of like an entertainment team.
start	V;PST;PL;1	started	We started it basically, because I wanted to - I wanted to work with Isabel on a lot of projects that we had going on together.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	We were coming with comics, like, left and right, we were coming up with things like Karis, in high school she came up with that one, I came up with an individual story called Thad's World Destruction and, she wanted to illustrate it, and so, that's the way we ended up doing it.
like	V;PRS;PL;1	like	Yep, we like to just make our own original content, and, yep, that's what we wanted to do.
meet	V;NFIN	meet	How did you two meet?
meet	V;PST;PL;1	met	We met back in high school.
keep	V;PST;SG;1	kept	But, before hand in middle school, I kept on hearing about this guy named Mario, and he kept on hearing about me, this girl named Isabel.
say	V;NFIN	say	And, everyone, all of our friends and all of our acquaintances would say, "Hey, you'd get along with him really well."
know	V;PST;SG;1	knew	So before hand I kinda knew about him, but I never met him.
introduce	V;PST;SG;3	introduced	And one of our friends, our common friends, he introduced us during study hall, and we just kind of hit it off from there.
involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	involved	Who else is involved in this project?
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's Jeff [Drake], who is behind the camera.
help	V;PRS;V.PTCP	helping	Right now, yeah, he's helping us right now.
talk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	talking	We're talking about doing some individual web series, video series.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	Josh Kindig, he's coming on board, he has some ideas for that too, he wants to take over "aywv" and do more gaming news.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's of course Jason, who is our editor and he's the one who writes dD.
meet	V;PST;V.PTCP	met	Well, back in college, back in Collins College, Isabel and I, we had met like she said at Sandia View Academy, and we went off to college together in Arizona;
explain	V;NFIN	explain	and ... (how do I best explain this?)
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Basically, she came up with a few class projects, and I came up with a few class projects;
start	V;PST;PL;1	started	we started working with a few of our friends, and colleagues and whatnot.
include	V;PST;PL;3	included	Those included Drew Cass, Devin Thurlow, people like that.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	She had several ideas, and she used to draw a lot in the sketchbook, she used to have this thick sketchbook that she used to carry around with her everywhere, where she'd draw these different characters and whatnot;
show	V;NFIN	show	and she was always really shy to show that off to people, and I wanted to help her show that stuff, and I wanted to bring it out to the world.
like	V;PST;SG;3	liked	I was a writer, and she always liked my writings, so I was like, let's just get both of our things together, and let's get this done.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	And for me, what I want the purpose to be, is to inspire people.
bring	V;NFIN	bring	To bring a little more light to the world is what I want, out of all of this.
have	V;PRS;PL;1	have	And what was the verse that we have?
want	V;PRS;PL;1	want	We just want to be able to bring, like she said, bring light into the entertainment and into the media industry, specifically is what we do.
know	V;NFIN	know	It was hot, as you yourself will know from your stay in Switzerland and the newspapers -- London was sweltering and the parks are not green, probably for the first time in two hundred years.
enjoy	V;PST;SG;3	enjoyed	Martha greatly enjoyed our stay in Mallorca at the villa of our friend Vane Ivanovic.
crowd	V;PST;V.PTCP	crowded	Luncheons and dinners were crowded with guests, but in the evenings she played bridge constantly and gossiped with our hostess.
present	V;PST;SG;3	presented	Vane himself presented me with five hundred pages of his memoirs when I arrived and, between sleeping and talking, I cut and edited the work: I am on holiday the inveterate and proverbial busman.
write	V;PST;SG;1	wrote	I wrote only about 2000 words on my novel, but I've finished now five chapters.
say	V;NFIN	say	It is romantic, and sad, which is to say, interesting experience recollected.
meet	V;PST;SG;1	met	At Vane's villa I met the Prince of Spain, Alphonso, who is the son of King Alphonso's deaf and mute son (the second one).
educate	V;PST;V.PTCP	educated	The Prince is married to Franco's granddaughter, a marvelously beautiful and seriously educated girl of twenty-six who is quite wasted on this royalist.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	I thought that in Mallorca I had at least escaped my duties as Regent, but Alphonso asked me about bilingual education for Puerto Ricans and beraded the Americans for suppressing their "fourteen million Latins."
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	He told me that Dean Acheson told him that Harry Truman denied Spain Marshall Plan aid because Truman was a Protestant.
argue	V;PST;SG;1	argued	When I argued that a lot ofAmerican felt deeply about the Spanish Civil War, and that the Marshall Plan was devised to help countries ravaged by the war, whereas Spain did not fight, he replied that this was a superficial answer.
fight	V;PST;V.PTCP	fought	Isn't it marvelous how wars, even when they are not fought, are found to betray everyone?
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	It is a quiet summer in New York, especially as there isn't such news except that of the city's financial troubles.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Odd, though, that there is none of the hectic spending and living here that seems to obtain amongst moneyed people and even the middle classes in London, where I was amazed to discover that the shops are full and places of entertainment are crowed.
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know what to make of it -- the idea that it shows the last days of Rome doesn't quite strike me as right.
fall	V;NFIN	fall	The pound will surely fallto $ 2.00 -- I've already started to discount our assets in London, although the operating revenues and profits there on our scientific books and journals are holding up quite apart from inflation in prices.
happen	V;PRS;V.PTCP	happening	What's happening in New York willhappen in some other cities around the world, I am convinced: Milan or Rome, Paris, Jakarta, and so forth.
miss	V;PRS;PL;1	miss	We miss you, Hannah, but we know that you must be getting some real rest.
go	V;NFIN	go	Will you go to Israel at the end of your stay?
be	V;PRS;SG;3	Is	Is there anything you need?
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	I've seen the doctor twice since I've been back, and he's convinced that I just missed an "episode," which means that I suffered what is always called, rather badly I think, a "coronary insufficiency."
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	I feel quite well now and I am watching myself.
lose	V;PST;SG;1	lost	I lost twelve pounds.
promise	V;PST;V.PTCP	promised	I've promised Dr. Steiner that I will not travel at all, andon humid days I stay at home and work and read.
reread	V;PRS;V.PTCP	rereading	I'm rereading Johnson's Lives of The Poets, a splendid work that proves that the best criticism is based on sensibility to art and prejudice to behavior.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	Mary is working away on her novel.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	I've been in touch with her briefly -- of course I saw her when she arrived in New York -- about reissuing Stones of Florence.
publish	V;PRS;V.PTCP	publishing	I am publishing the Chiaramonte book in the Spring.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Mary has provided a preface.
proceed	V;NFIN	proceed	How does The Life of the Mind proceed?
look	V;PRS;SG;1	look	I look forward to going over manuscript with you when you return.
recall	V;NFIN	recall	We were, as you will recall, interrupted when we were working at Aberdeen.
send	V;PRS;PL;1	send	Hannah, Martha and I send you love and greetings and we hope that the summer ends with lazy leisurely days for you.
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	We shall be seeing you soon.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	I hope all is well in the Office of Undergraduate Education, and thank you for scheduling time to meet with me regarding the Penn State Wiki proposal.
paint	V;PRS;SG;3	paints	The overall proposal paints broad strokes in defining how wiki technology can be useful at Penn State; I look forward to productive conversation with your office focusing on one aspect of the proposal: student-based course descriptions.
submit	V;PST;V.PTCP	submitted	In three letters submitted to the Office of University Relations and Division of Student Affairs, I defend wiki technology as a means to improve overall University communications.
outline	V;PRS;PL;3	outline	The letters outline faculty and student benefits, editing fundamentals, online legal protections, information quality and reliability, and promotion of free speech in an online setting.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	The purpose of this letter is to provide appropriate background to facilitate our 9-May-2006 meeting.
object	V;PRS;PL;3	object	While University Relations and Student Affairs object to University-sponsored public wikis on the grounds that it creates a "risk of false information going out to too many people before it can be corrected," in addition to the University having a "certain respect for fact checking and the truth" and "responsibility to maintaining accurate information," 1 I posit that the rational lacks merit in light of individuals being able to judge for themselves what information is trustworthy on a wiki.
acknowledge	V;PRS;SG;1	acknowledge	I acknowledge the University's responsibility to maintain accurate information on official Web pages, however, the University policy entitled "Copyright Information” 2 reads as follows (emphasis added):
decide	V;PRS;SG;3	decides	"Penn State decides against supporting wiki project."
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	presented	The information presented on Penn State's Web site comes from a variety of sources, including not only official Penn State departments and colleges, but also unofficial sources and individuals.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Although every effort is made to present current and accurate information, the University does not author or edit all of the pages presented here and therefore cannot assume responsibility for all information.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	The author of each Web page is responsible for the content of that page and is expected to abide by University policies and local, state, and federal laws.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	The Penn State Wiki was never proposed as a source of official information, and the University already hosts non-official content that is highly circulated yet occasionally incorrect (e.g., The Daily Collegian).
decrease	V;PRS;SG;3	decreases	Additionally, higher circulation of a wiki article decreases the likelihood it will contain misinformation, a design advantage not present in other technologies such as newspapers, radio, and most internet sites.
commit	V;PST;V.PTCP	committed	As a volunteer editor for the Wikimedia Foundation, I am committed to ensuring that posted information is as accurate and reputable as possible by checking facts and verifying sources.
expect	V;PRS;SG;1	expect	As such, I expect the same of other Wikimedia Foundation contributors and also intend for the standard of expectation to carry over into University-hosted wikis.
publish	V;PST;SG;1	published	I published video material on the Undergraduate Student Government (U.S.G.) website 3 addressing benefits of wiki technology, and I recorded individual commentary.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	The following is a partial transcript from one videocast entitled "Introduction to the Penn State Wiki":
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Student--based class descriptions benefit Penn State two--fold.
enroll	V;PST;V.PTCP	enrolled	The first is the ability for current students enrolled in the class to provide content descriptions for other students considering taking the same course, for those students to have a better idea of what to expect in terms choosing the best academic course for their needs.
offer	V;NFIN	offer	Penn State currently doesn't offer very comprehensive information on any of its 15,000--plus courses.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	The second reason student--based class descriptions are beneficial to Penn State are in providing instructors and other administrative officials with more up--to--date and comprehensive feedback than they would normally be able to receive through traditional methods such as surveys.
have	V;NFIN	have	The idea is for Penn State to have enough input to intelligently benefit and improve upon each of their 15,000 courses.
maintain	V;PRS;SG;3	maintains	Penn State still maintains control of each of the courses, so the academic standard is kept just as high as it always has been.
improve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	improving	This is the area Undergraduate Student Government is most passionate about in terms of improving the operation of the University.
suggest	V;PRS;SG;1	suggest	I suggest instituting a pilot program to evaluate the effectiveness of wikis in providing student-based course descriptions, with one wiki assigned to each academic unit.
volunteer	V;NFIN	volunteer	Under the pilot program, faculty members would be able to volunteer their classes for participation, and any person with University affiliation would be able to edit any of the descriptions.
attract	V;NFIN	attract	The pilot program, if successful, should attract additional faculty members and encourage departmental participation; the program would remain manageable while being scaled into widespread usage.
look	V;PRS;SG;1	look	I look forward to our discussion on Tuesday, and would be more than willing to elaborate on any of the areas that interest you during or after our meeting.
enclose	V;PST;V.PTCP	encl	encl: U.S.G. Joint Resolution 36-01: Penn State Wiki
prosecute	V;PST;V.PTCP	prosecuted	Parents prosecuted after homeopathic treatment leads to daughter's death
undergo	V;PRS;V.PTCP	undergoing	Thomas Sam, 42, and his wife Manju Sam, 36, from Sydney, Australia, are undergoing trial for manslaughter by gross negligence for the death of their nine-month-old child, Gloria.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	She died from infection caused by severe eczema after they shunned effective conventional medical treatments for homeopathy, a form of alternative medicine that has been described as pseudoscience.
review	V;PST;V.PTCP	reviewed	Articles in peer-reviewed academic journals including Social Science & Medicine have characterized homeopathy as a form of quackery.
develop	V;PST;SG;3	developed	Gloria developed severe eczema at the age of four months and the parents were advised to send the child to a skin specialist.
practise	V;PRS;V.PTCP	practising	Thomas Sam, a practising homeopath, instead decided to treat his daughter himself.
deteriorate	V;PST;SG;3	deteriorated	His daughter's condition deteriorated, to the point that the baby spent all her energy battling the infections caused by the constant breaking of the skin, leading to severe malnutrition and, eventually, her death.
break	V;PST;SG;3	broke	By the end, Gloria's eczema was so severe that her skin broke every time her parents changed her clothes or nappy, and in the words of the Crown prosecutor, Mark Tedeschi, QC, "Gloria spent a lot of the last five months of her life crying, irritable, scratching and the only thing that gave her solace was to suck on her mother's breast."
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	Gloria also became unable to move her legs.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	Mr. Tedeschi also told the court that, over the last five months of her life, "Gloria's eczema played a devastating role in her overall health and it is asserted by the Crown that both her parents knew this and discussed it with each other."
continue	V;PST;PL;3	continued	However, despite their child's severe illness, and her lack of improvement, the Sams continued to shun conventional medical treatment, instead seeking help from other homeopaths and naturopaths.
improve	V;PST;SG;3	improved	Gloria temporarily improved during the rare times they used conventional treatments, but they soon dropped them in favour of homeopathy, and she consistently worsened.
plead	V;PST;SG;3	pleaded	Allegedly, Thomas' sister pleaded with him to send Gloria to a conventional medical doctor, but he replied "I am not able to do that".
accuse	V;PST;V.PTCP	accused	The parents are also accused of putting their social life ahead of their child, taking her on a trip to India and leaving her to servants while embarking on a busy social schedule, and giving her homeopathic drops instead of using the prescription creams they had been given.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	Gloria was finally taken to the emergency department shortly before her death.
weep	V;PRS;V.PTCP	weeping	By this time, "her skin was weeping, her body malnourished and her corneas melting", according to the Sydney Morning Herald.
speak	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Speaking	Speaking in the parents' defense, Tom Molomby, SC, said that, as the parents came from India, where homeopathy is in common use, they should be declared not guilty due to cultural differences.
treat	V;PRS;SG;3	treats	Homeopathy is a form of alternative medicine which treats patients with massively diluted forms of substances that, if given to a healthy person undiluted, would cause symptoms similar to the disease.
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Typical treatments take the dilutions, with ritualised shaking between each step of the dilution, past the level where any molecules of the original substance are likely to remain; for homeopathic treatments to work, basic well-understood concepts in chemistry and physics would have to be wrong.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is no evidence that homeopathy is more effective than placebo for any condition.
suffer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	suffering	Australian children suffering from iodine deficiency
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	Almost half of all Australian primary school children are mild to moderately iodine deficient, researchers say.
document	V;PRS;V.PTCP	documenting	A new study documenting iodine nutritional status in Australian school children has revealed many are not getting enough iodine - which can lead to mental and growth retardation.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	The report's authors say iodine deficiency is "the sleeper health issue in Australia", and potentially a very serious one.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	The results of the Australian National Iodine Nutrition Study published in the Medical Journal of Australia this week, revealed that children in mainland Australia are borderline iodine deficient.
prompt	V;PST;V.PTCP	prompted	The report has prompted calls for all edible salt to be iodised.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	They say adding the mineral to salt is the simplest and most effective method of preventing iodine deficiency disorders.
carry	V;PST;V.PTCP	carried	A cross-sectional survey of 1709 schoolchildren - aged 8–10 years, from 88 schools - was carried out in New South Wales, Victoria, South Australia, Western Australia and Queensland, between July 2003 and December 2004.
exclude	V;PST;V.PTCP	excluded	Tasmania was excluded from the study - where an voluntary iodine fortification program using iodised salt in bread, is ongoing.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	The authors say the results confirm the existence of inadequate iodine intake in the Australian population.
call	V;PRS;PL;3	call	They call for "urgent implementation of mandatory iodisation of all edible salt in Australia."
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	Most iodine in food comes from seafood, milk and iodised salt.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Professor Cres Eastman, Director of the National Iodine Nutrition study, and Chairman of the Australian Centre for Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders, says it is crucial that children and pregnant women in particular have an adequate intake of iodine.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	Iodine deficiency can lead to serious health problems including brain damage, stunted growth and deafness.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Professor Eastman says manufacturers could easily remedy the situation by using iodised salt in their products in line with the United States and most European countries.
suspect	V;PRS;SG;1	suspect	"I suspect they won't do that on a voluntary basis, we've tried so far and haven't succeeded, so we've convinced the Food Standards of Australia and New Zealand | that all salt should be iodised," he said.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The report says the decline in iodine intake appears to be due to changes in the dairy industry, where chlorine-containing sanitisers have replaced iodine-containing sanitisers.
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	Iodine released from these chemicals into milk has been the major source of dietary iodine in Australia for at least four decades, but is now declining.
decrease	V;PRS;V.PTCP	decreasing	Another contributory factor has been the decreasing consumption of iodised salt used in foods.
state	V;PRS;SG;3	states	The report states that few if any food manufacturers use iodised salt in the preparation and manufacture of foods.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Professor Eastman says iodine is added to only 10 per cent of Australian salt in contravention of a World Health Organisation recommendation that all salt be iodised.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	He says authorities are reacting slowly to his urgent calls for mandatory iodised salt.
occur	V;PRS;SG;3	occurs	"The effects of iodine deficiency are dependent upon how severe it is and when it occurs.
go	V;PRS;PL;1	go	So if we go to the pregnant woman, she doesn't get enough iodine, she won't make enough thyroid hormone, and the foetus won't get the amount of thyroid hormone it needs for adequate and proper development of the brain, so you'll then see consequences being loss of IQ, learning difficulties, hearing difficulties and other neurological problems," Professor Eastman said.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	"If an infant's not getting enough iodine ... brain development won't be completed and they won't grow normally, and as you get older the problem will be that you will develop a goiter and your thyroid won't function as well as it should, so that may have all sorts of pernicious effects upon normal function in life."
live	V;PRS;PL;3	live	More than two billion people around the world live in areas prone to iodine deficiency, and yet the problem is easily fixed.
recommend	V;PRS;SG;3	recommends	The World Health Organisation (WHO) recommends that every country should iodise all edible salt.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The most well known effects of IDD are visible goiter and cretinism, a condition characterised by severe brain damage occurring in very early life.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	WHO say Iodine deficiency is the world’s most prevalent, yet easily preventable, cause of brain damage.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Professor Eastman said he is alarmed by what they found.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	"Pregnant women in Australia are getting about half as much as what they require on a daily basis.
alarm	V;PRS;SG;3	alarms	So that alarms me, because there's quite serious potential for adverse effects and brain damage in the next generation of children born in this country," he said.
lose	V;PRS;2;SG	lose	"If Iodine deficiency is serious you lose 15 IQ points, on average.
be	V;NFIN	be	There shouldn't be anyone suffering from iodine deficiency in a developed country like Australia."
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Lydia Buchtmann for Food Standards Australia New Zealand, says they are looking at mandatory guidelines on iodine by the end of the year.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	She says the issue is complicated and will take time to get right.
need	V;PRS;PL;1	need	We need to "make sure there's sufficient iodine added into the food supply, to help those people with a deficiency.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	But at the other end of the scale we've got to make sure the people who eat a lot of food - we all know the teenage boy who comes home from school and eats a whole loaf of bread - that those people don't get too much and get overdose," Ms Bauchtman said.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	"One of the reasons that iodine is going down is because people are taking that good healthy eating message and not adding salt during cooking."
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Senior researcher Mu Li, of the University of NSW's school of public health, said "it is reasonable to assume that pregnant women and breastfeeding mothers are also iodine deficient, putting the next generation of children born in this country at risk of the neuropsychological consequences of iodine deficiency."
hear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hearing	What you're hearing is Mangal Shobhajatra, an annual procession of thousands of people that takes over the streets of Bangladesh's capital city, Dhaka.
mark	V;PRS;SG;3	marks	It marks the start of the Bengali New Year, and as you might expect of a New Year's celebration, it's a very festive affair.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Not long ago, UNSECO, which is the educational, scientific and cultural agency of the United Nations, named this parade intangible cultural heritage.
look	V;NFIN	look	But what might look like just a street party is actually a creative stand for unity and against the forces of intolerance who seek to divide and oppress Bangladeshis.
dig	V;PRS;PL;1	dig	Welcome to Into the Deep, The Global Voices podcast, where we dig deep into one topic that isn't getting the media coverage it deserves.
live	V;PRS;SG;1	live	I live in Bilbao, Spain.
manage	V;PRS;V.PTCP	managing	And I'm Sahar, managing editor at Global Voices.
dig	V;PRS;V.PTCP	digging	I'm currently in Lahore, Pakistan, and today we are digging deep into Bangladesh's Mangal Shobhajatra.
sweep	V;PST;V.PTCP	swept	The tide of xenophobia has seemingly swept Europe and the United States in recent years, culminating in 2016 with the UK's vote to leave the European Union and the election of Donald Trump in the US.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	From Myanmar to South Africa to Indonesia, there are those who view diversity as a source of fear rather than strength.
disagree	V;NFIN	disagree	Do you disagree with this trend?
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	And are you thinking about what to do to combat these forces that want to sow the seeds of division?
offer	V;NFIN	offer	Perhaps then Bangladesh and its unique New Year's procession can offer some inspiration.
go	V;NFIN	go	How does it go?
pronounce	V;PRS;PL;1	pronounce	It's -- in Bangla we always pronounce o, so it's a a --
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Although it is written in English as M A N, but it sounds more like Mongol.
say	V;IMP;2	Say	Say it again very slowly.
want	V;NFIN	want	We don't want to mess it up.
shout	V;PRS;V.PTCP	shouting	It's it’s very loud, with people shouting, shouting different slogans and, uh, they're singing, they're dancing and people participate with uh joy.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	writing	He's a copywriter and a member of the Global Voices community, who's been writing for us now for several years.
speak	V;PST;PL;1	spoke	We spoke to both Pantha and Rezwan to learn more about Mangal Shobhajatra.
start	V;PRS;SG;3	starts	Yeah, it it all starts with making of masks.
prepare	V;PRS;PL;3	prepare	Actually, the students of uh the Charukola Institute, which is the, uh, Faculty of Fine Arts of Dhaka University and uh they prepare all these, uh, all kinds of interesting masks, and they are very colorful, and they bear animals and demons and mythical mythical faces and all.
carry	V;PRS;PL;3	carry	And people carry those masks, uh, in, in batons, or they put it on their face and they, they dance and, uh, on the way there are like many street shops, uh, for food and, and and balloons, children are running around.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	The procession takes place every year on the morning of the first day of the Bengali calendar, which is April 14th or 15th.
organize	V;PRS;PL;3	organize	Students and teachers of Dhaka University's Faculty of Fine Arts organize it and anyone is free to join in.
attend	V;PST;V.PTCP	attended	Have either of you attended?
go	V;NFIN	go	Um, it's one of the go go to things that -- uh, during the New Year celebration.
have	V;PRS;PL;1	have	So we have a kind of ritual.
go	V;PRS;PL;1	go	So, uh, on the first day of the new year, we go in the morning to listen to, uh, Rabindra Sangeet.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Rabindra Sangeet or Tagore songs or popular songs that were written in the late 19th and early 20th centuries by a man named Rabindranath Tagore.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	They're often based on Hindustani classical music and folk music of Bengal.
win	V;NFIN	win	An interesting fact, Rabindranath Tagore was the first non-European to win the Nobel Prize in Literature.
happen	V;PST;SG;3	happened	Well, it happened folks.
happen	V;PST;SG;3	happened	WrestleMania happened, Raw happened, Smackdown happened, everything fucking happened.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Holy shit, there is so much to talk about this week, and we’re going to cover it all, the best we can.
join	V;PST;V.PTCP	joined	I’m your host as always, Dave, and I am joined by Alex.
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	We were supposed to have word from Carl.
know	V;NFIN	know	We don’t know where he is, so I’ll just take that as a – he’s one of the people that is cutting his wrists because the Undertaker’s career is over!
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Those of you who take offense to that, fuck off and –
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	What did, uh, what’s his name – what did, uh, our, our, wh-, the one that always is dressed up like Undertaker on Facebook?
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	Yeah, he did the exact same thing, he folded his clothes and put ’em on or something.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	Yeah, I thought I saw that picture, what does it look like, what is he – what is he doing?
ignore	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ignoring	I’m ignoring anybody who is taking this retirement personally and acting like this is the end of the fucking world.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	Um, I’m gonna say this right now, and I’m gonna say it again when we get to the match – I’m glad it’s over.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	And I know people that are getting offended by that.
look	V;NFIN	Look	Look, I I can just – look, I fucking cried when – I fucking cried when – but it’s OK, it’s it’s not the end of the world.
watch	V;NFIN	watch	You can still watch wrestling, it’s not “oh, he retired; that’s it, I’m done, I can’t watch it anymore.”
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, for God’s sakes, he was only there like –
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, how many days are in a year?
come	V;IMP;2	c’m	365 days, so he was there one out of – c’mon.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	He made two appearances.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, I mean, OK, think about it like this: at Wrestlemania 31 he was there once the whole year, cause he was only there the day of Wrestlemania.
watch	V;PST;2;SG	watched	So that’s one day of the year that you watched him, that year.
miss	V;PRS;V.PTCP	missing	Are you really missing that much of wrestling by not having Undertaker there?
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, come on.
come	V;NFIN	come	Yeah, and he’ll be mine as well, but there does come a time when you have to go “look, it’s over”.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean the guy – the guy – the guy can’t wrestle forever.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean he’s – he was, 53?
wrestle	V;PST;SG;3	wrestled	Yeah that was not -- he wrestled like a 75,000 year-old man.
get	V;PRS;PL;1	get	Yeah, when we get to that match I’m gonna be so critical on it.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	And I know people are gonna be pissed off at me.
care	V;NFIN	care	And I don’t care, I’m gonna call it like I see it, the rose colored glasses are off, the nostalgia is gone.
stop	V;IMP;2	Stop	Stop wanting the fucking 90s back, because the 90s are dead, get the fuck over it.
stop	V;IMP;2	stop	You people and your fucking 90s nostalgia, stop it.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let the 90s fucking die.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let it fucking die, please, just stop.
stop	V;IMP;2	Stop	Stop.
remember	V;NFIN	remember	Um, it was – like, I don’t even remember – well, let me see.
forget	V;PST;SG;1	forgot	I forgot to mention, I guess I should mention this now.
let	V;NFIN	let	This is actually not from this week but, let me see.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let me look at the calendar real quick on the computer.
let	V;PRS;V.PTCP	letting	And you’re just now letting us know?
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	belie-	Well I belie- I think I was wished happy birthday by all of you on Facebook, so, that’s fine.
think	V;NFIN	think	But I didn’t – but I don’t think we’ve had a show since, cause we – cause we – cause we missed so many shows in a row.
put	V;PST;SG;1	put	But uh, yeah, I put a video up on YouTube.
drink	V;PST;PL;1	drank	We all drank a whole lotta shit.
get	V;NFIN	get	Um, I didn’t get drunk at all despite literally shotgunning I think six or seven shots of … was it tequila? .. yeah, I think it was like really really decent tequila, in a row.
feel	V;NFIN	feel	I didn’t feel anything, cause I don’t know that’s just how I am.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	But yeah, that’s up there on my YouTube channel, not that anybody ever goes on it.
know	V;NFIN	know	So uh, this week I don’t know, they added more DLC, the only DLC for King of Fighters.
say	V;NFIN	say	Did you say you liked Rock Howard?
forget	V;PRS;SG;1	forget	I forget.
mind	V;NFIN	mind	I don’t mind Rock Howard.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Um I think he’s cool.
