take	V;NFIN	take	Is Iori in there or did they take him out?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	It’s Iori I think, or Yori?
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	No, I know who Rugal is.
swear	V;PRS;SG;1	swear	Madam Speaker, "I solemnly swear that in all things appertaining to the trial of the impeachment of Donald John Trump, President of the United States, now pending, I will do impartial justice according to the Constitution and laws."
swear	V;PST;PL;3	swore	That is the oath Senators swore on January 16.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	It is the oath created by Senators when they tried the first impeachment of a President in 1868.
root	V;PST;V.PTCP	rooted	It is an oath rooted in the Revolution fought by their grandparents to create a republic of laws, not kings.
derive	V;PRS;SG;3	derives	It is an oath whose power derives from its common sense: that a juror must always be impartial for a trial to be fair.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	And it is an oath made necessary by the fact that Senators are not, as we are not, under normal circumstances, impartial in our work.
choose	V;PST;V.PTCP	chosen	The words chosen for this oath recognize that when our Constitution calls Senators to try impeachment, it calls them away from their role as partisans.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	When that oath is taken, Senators are supposed to step back from the affiliation of party or political kinship with or opposition to the President on trial.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	They are required, as the oath plainly states, to "do impartial justice according to the Constitution and laws."
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Madam Speaker, this afternoon, Senators will be asked to vote on the two Articles of Impeachment the House presented on abuse of power and the obstruction of Congress.
vote	V;PRS;V.PTCP	voting	After voting to refuse to hear evidence and call witnesses with pertinent information, nearly all Republican Senators have already announced that they will vote against the articles.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	In doing so, many of them acknowledge that what President Trump did was wrong and inappropriate.
accept	V;PRS;PL;3	accept	They accept that it was wrong for him to withhold military aid to Ukraine until the President of that country promised to interfere in the American elections.
solicit	V;NFIN	solicit	The evidence of President Trump's abuse of power and attempt to solicit foreign interference in the 2020 elections is clear enough that Republican Senators cannot and have not denied the facts, yet they cannot bring themselves to confront this President and are choosing party over country.
explain	V;PRS;V.PTCP	explaining	The Senator from Alaska, in explaining her decision to vote to block witnesses and evidence, tried to deflect responsibility from the consequences of her actions, writing: "I have come to the conclusion that there will be no fair trial in the Senate."
agree	V;PRS;SG;1	agree	I agree with that.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	She further said: "It is sad for me today to admit that, as an institution, the Congress has failed."
fail	V;PST;V.PTCP	failed	Madam Speaker, the Congress has not failed.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	The House did its job, whether you agree or not.
impeach	V;PST;PL;1	impeached	In regular order, by a vote of this House, we impeached the President of the United States based upon our oath to protect and defend the Constitution of the United States.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	The House did its job and did so with the solemnity required when undertaking the process of impeachment, which we did not seek but accepted as our responsibility under the Constitution.
hold	V;PST;PL;1	held	We held hearings, called witnesses, and subpoenaed documents.
withhold	V;PST;V.PTCP	withheld	Many of the witnesses and documents, of course, were withheld by the White House.
fail	V;NFIN	fail	It is the Senate that will fail if Senators do not uphold their oaths to impartial justice.
fail	V;NFIN	fail	It is the Senate, Madam Speaker, that will fail if it does not hold this President accountable for using a hold on military aid to compel an ally to interfere in our election for his own personal gain.
judge	V;NFIN	judge	History will judge poorly those who choose fear of their party over the courage to do the right thing.
urge	V;PST;PL;1	urged	Neither the Speaker nor myself, nor the whip, Jim Clyburn, urged any member in our party to vote any way on impeachment.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was no lobbying.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was no pressure.
vote	V;PST;PL;3	voted	Our members voted consistent with their oath of office and the conviction that that vote was required by that oath to protect and defend the Constitution.
judge	V;NFIN	judge	Americans will judge.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	I am often asked why the House passed Articles of Impeachment even knowing that the odds were slim that Senate Republicans would set aside partisanship and hear the case as impartial jurors.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	It is because I know future generations will look back on this chapter in our history and ask:
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	Who stood up for the Constitution and the laws?
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	Who stood up for the values our Founders charged us to keep?
refuse	V;PST;SG;3	refused	Who refused to shrink from the heavy responsibilities of their oath?
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	I can be proud that the House did its job, followed the law, defended our Constitution.
convict	V;NFIN	convict	We did not convict; that is not our role.
say	V;PST;PL;1	said	Essentially, what we said was there was probable cause that powers had been abused and certainly cause to see that the President refused to cooperate with the constitutional responsibilities of the House of Representatives.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	I am also proud of the House managers, as all of my colleagues on the Democratic side of the aisle are proud of our managers who made their case.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	They made their case with intellect.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	They made their case with evidence that had been adduced here in the House.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	They made their case and appealed to Senators to hold this President accountable, as our Founders intended.
watch	V;PST;V.PTCP	watched	Almost everybody has watched a trial either in person or on television.
open	V;PRS;V.PTCP	opening	A trial is not an opening argument and a closing argument with nothing in between.
want	V;PST;SG;3	wanted	Seventy-five percent of our people wanted to have witnesses because that was their understanding of what a trial is, not just argument at the beginning and argument at the end, but evidence for jurors who have pledged to be impartial to consider.
agree	V;NFIN	agree	Any judge in this country would agree that opening and closing statements alone are not a trial.
prove	V;PST;PL;3	proved	Nevertheless, the House managers proved their case.
know	V;PRS;PL;3	know	The American people know what that truth is and know what this President has done.
remember	V;NFIN	remember	And they will remember who on this day abided by the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	The orderly transfer of authority as called for in the Constitution routinely takes place, as it has for almost two centuries, and few of us stop to think how unique we really are.
accept	V;PRS;PL;1	accept	In the eyes of many in the world, this every 4-year ceremony we accept as normal is nothing less than a miracle.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	Mr. President, I want our fellow citizens to know how much you did to carry on this tradition.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	By your gracious cooperation in the transition process, you have shown a watching world that we are a united people pledged to maintaining a political system which guarantees individual liberty to a greater degree than any other, and I thank you and your people for all your help in maintaining the continuity which is the bulwark of our Republic.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	The business of our nation goes forward.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	These United States are confronted with an economic affliction of great proportions.
suffer	V;PRS;PL;1	suffer	We suffer from the longest and one of the worst sustained inflations in our national history.
distort	V;PRS;SG;3	distorts	It distorts our economic decisions, penalizes thrift, and crushes the struggling young and the fixed-income elderly alike.
threaten	V;PRS;SG;3	threatens	It threatens to shatter the lives of millions of our people.
cast	V;PST;V.PTCP	cast	Idle industries have cast workers into unemployment, human misery, and personal indignity.
work	V;NFIN	work	Those who do work are denied a fair return for their labor by a tax system which penalizes successful achievement and keeps us from maintaining full productivity.
keep	V;PST;V.PTCP	kept	But great as our tax burden is, it has not kept pace with public spending.
pile	V;PST;V.PTCP	piled	For decades we have piled deficit upon deficit, mortgaging our future and our children's future for the temporary convenience of the present.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	To continue this long trend is to guarantee tremendous social, cultural, political, and economic upheavals.
borrow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	borrowing	You and I, as individuals, can, by borrowing, live beyond our means, but for only a limited period of time.
think	V;NFIN	think	Why, then, should we think that collectively, as a nation, we're not bound by that same limitation?
act	V;NFIN	act	We must act today in order to preserve tomorrow.
let	V;IMP;2	let	And let there be no misunderstanding: We are going to begin to act, beginning today.
suffer	V;PRS;PL;1	suffer	The economic ills we suffer have come upon us over several decades.
go	V;NFIN	go	They will not go away in days, weeks, or months, but they will go away.
go	V;NFIN	go	They will go away because we as Americans have the capacity now, as we've had in the past, to do whatever needs to be done to preserve this last and greatest bastion of freedom.
tempt	V;PST;V.PTCP	tempted	From time to time we've been tempted to believe that society has become too complex to be managed by self-rule, that government by an elite group is superior to government for, by, and of the people.
govern	V;PRS;V.PTCP	governing	Well, if no one among us is capable of governing himself, then who among us has the capacity to govern someone else?
bear	V;NFIN	bear	All of us together, in and out of government, must bear the burden.
seek	V;PRS;PL;1	seek	The solutions we seek must be equitable, with no one group singled out to pay a higher price.
hear	V;PRS;PL;1	hear	We hear much of special interest groups.
neglect	V;PST;V.PTCP	neglected	Well, our concern must be for a special interest group that has been too long neglected.
know	V;PRS;SG;3	knows	It knows no sectional boundaries or ethnic and racial divisions, and it crosses political party lines.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	It is made up of men and women who raise our food, patrol our streets, man our mines and factories, teach our children, keep our homes, and heal us when we're sick — professionals, industrialists, shopkeepers, clerks, cabbies, and truck drivers.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	They are, in short, "We the people," this breed called Americans.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	Well, this administration's objective will be a healthy, vigorous, growing economy that provides equal opportunities for all Americans, with no barriers born of bigotry or discrimination.
put	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Putting	Putting America back to work means putting all Americans back to work.
end	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Ending	Ending inflation means freeing all Americans from the terror of runaway living costs.
share	V;NFIN	share	All must share in the productive work of this "new beginning," and all must share in the bounty of a revived economy.
have	V;NFIN	have	With the idealism and fair play which are the core of our system and our strength, we can have a strong and prosperous America, at peace with itself and the world.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The government of the United States can best be described as a republic, or representative democracy.
rest	V;PRS;SG;3	rests	A democracy is a government in which political power—influence over institutions, leaders, and policies—rests in the hands of the people.
govern	V;NFIN	govern	In a representative democracy, however, the citizens do not govern directly.
elect	V;PRS;PL;3	elect	Instead, they elect representatives to make decisions and pass laws on behalf of all the people.
vote	V;PRS;PL;3	vote	Thus, U.S. citizens vote for members of Congress, the president and vice president, members of state legislatures, governors, mayors, and members of town councils and school boards to act on their behalf.
favor	V;PRS;PL;3	favor	Most representative governments favor majority rule:
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	the opinions of the majority of the people have more influence with government than those of the minority.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	If the number of elected representatives who favor a proposed law is greater than those who oppose it, the law will be enacted.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	However, in representative governments like the United States, minority rights are protected: people cannot be deprived of certain rights even if an overwhelming number of people think that they should be.
let	V;IMP;2	let	For example, let’s say American society decided that atheists, people who do not believe that God exists, were evil and should be imprisoned or expelled from the country.
account	V;PRS;PL;3	account	Even though atheists only account for about 7 percent of the population, they would be protected due to minority rights. 4
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	Even though the number of Americans who believe in God far outweighs the number who do not, the minority is still protected.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Because decisions are made through majority rule, making your opinions known and voting for those men and women who make decisions that affect all of us are critical and influential forms of civic engagement in a representative democracy such as the United States.
participate	V;PRS;PL;3	participate	In a direct democracy, unlike representative democracy, people participate directly in making government decisions.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	For example, in ancient Athens, the most famous example of a direct democracy, all male citizens were allowed to attend meetings of the Assembly.
debate	V;PST;PL;3	debated	Here they debated and voted for or against all proposed laws.
function	V;PRS;PL;3	function	Although neither the federal government nor any of the state governments function as a direct democracy—the Constitution requires the national and state governments to be representative forms of government—some elements of direct democracy do exist in the United States.
vote	V;PRS;PL;3	vote	While residents of the different states vote for people to represent them and to make laws in their behalf in the state legislatures and in Congress, people may still directly vote on certain issues.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	For example, a referendum or proposed law might be placed on the ballot for citizens to vote on directly during state or local elections instead of leaving the matter in the hands of the state legislature.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	At New England town meetings, all residents are allowed to debate decisions affecting the town (Figure 1.5).
provide	V;PRS;PL;3	provide	Such occasions provide additional opportunities for civic engagement.
gather	V;PRS;PL;3	gather	Residents of Boxborough, Massachusetts, gather in a local hotel to discuss issues affecting their town.
provide	V;PRS;PL;3	provide	New England town meetings provide an opportunity for people to experience direct democracy.
last	V;PST;V.PTCP	lasted	This tradition has lasted for hundreds of years.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Most countries now have some form of representative government (Figure 1.6). 5
drive	V;PST;V.PTCP	driven	At the other end of the political spectrum are elite-driven forms of government.
hold	V;PRS;SG;3	holds	In a monarchy, one ruler, usually a hereditary ruler, holds political power.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	Although the power of some monarchs is limited by law, and such kings and queens often rule along with an elected legislature that makes laws for the country, this is not always the case.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Many southwest Asian kingdoms, such as Saudi Arabia, Qatar, and the United Arab Emirates, have absolute monarchs whose power is unrestricted.
discuss	V;PST;V.PTCP	discussed	As discussed earlier, another nondemocratic form of government is oligarchy, in which a handful of elite members of society, often those who belong to a particular political party, hold all political power.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	For example, in Cuba, as in China, only members of the Communist Party are allowed to vote or hold public office, and the party’s most important members make all government decisions.
control	V;PRS;SG;3	controls	Under totalitarianism, the government is more important than the citizens, and it controls all aspects of citizens’ lives.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	Citizens’ rights are limited, and the government does not allow political criticism or opposition.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	The map of the world shows the different forms of government that currently exist.
color	V;PST;V.PTCP	colored	Countries that are colored blue have some form of representative democracy, although the people may not have as much political power as they do in the United States.
color	V;PST;V.PTCP	colored	Countries that are colored red, like China, Vietnam, and Cuba, have an oligarchic form of government.
color	V;PST;V.PTCP	colored	Countries that are colored yellow are monarchies where the people play little part in governing.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The CIA website provides information about the types of government across the world.
increase	V;PRS;SG;3	Increases	Why the Division of Labor Increases Production
divide	V;PRS;PL;1	divide	When we divide and subdivide the tasks involved with producing a good or service, workers and businesses can produce a greater quantity of output.
notice	V;PST;SG;3	noticed	In his observations of pin factories, Smith noticed that one worker alone might make 20 pins in a day, but that a small business of 10 workers (some of whom would need to complete two or three of the 18 tasks involved with pin-making), could make 48,000 pins in a day.
specialize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	specializing	How can a group of workers, each specializing in certain tasks, produce so much more than the same number of workers who try to produce the entire good or service by themselves?
offer	V;PST;SG;3	offered	Smith offered three reasons.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	First, specialization in a particular small job allows workers to focus on the parts of the production process where they have an advantage.
develop	V;NFIN	develop	(In later chapters, we will develop this idea by discussing comparative advantage.)
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	People have different skills, talents, and interests, so they will be better at some jobs than at others.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	The particular advantages may be based on educational choices, which are in turn shaped by interests and talents.
qualify	V;PRS;PL;3	qualify	Only those with medical degrees qualify to become doctors, for instance.
affect	V;PRS;SG;3	affects	For some goods, geography affects specialization.
run	V;NFIN	run	For example, it is easier to be a wheat farmer in North Dakota than in Florida, but easier to run a tourist hotel in Florida than in North Dakota.
live	V;PRS;2;SG	live	If you live in or near a big city, it is easier to attract enough customers to operate a successful dry cleaning business or movie theater than if you live in a sparsely populated rural area.
specialize	V;PRS;PL;3	specialize	Whatever the reason, if people specialize in the production of what they do best, they will be more effective than if they produce a combination of things, some of which they are good at and some of which they are not.
specialize	V;PRS;PL;3	specialize	Second, workers who specialize in certain tasks often learn to produce more quickly and with higher quality.
hold	V;PRS;SG;3	holds	This pattern holds true for many workers, including assembly line laborers who build cars, stylists who cut hair, and doctors who perform heart surgery.
know	V;PRS;PL;3	know	In fact, specialized workers often know their jobs well enough to suggest innovative ways to do their work faster and better.
operate	V;PRS;SG;3	operates	A similar pattern often operates within businesses.
focus	V;PRS;SG;3	focuses	In many cases, a business that focuses on one or a few products (sometimes called its “core competency”) is more successful than firms that try to make a wide range of products.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	Third, specialization allows businesses to take advantage of economies of scale, which means that for many goods, as the level of production increases, the average cost of producing each individual unit declines.
produce	V;PRS;SG;3	produces	For example, if a factory produces only 100 cars per year, each car will be quite expensive to make on average.
produce	V;PRS;SG;3	produces	However, if a factory produces 50,000 cars each year, then it can set up an assembly line with huge machines and workers performing specialized tasks, and the average cost of production per car will be lower.
focus	V;NFIN	focus	The ultimate result of workers who can focus on their preferences and talents, learn to do their specialized jobs better, and work in larger organizations is that society as a whole can produce and consume far more than if each person tried to produce all of his or her own goods and services.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	My name is Katie and today I'm going to be talking about my two and a half week long vacation to Portland, Oregon.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	So I am from Washington D.C., and I have a bunch of family in Portland, Oregon, so it wasn't my first time going there.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	I thought that I would make sort of like more of an informative video because I could just like post a vlog, but um the footage was like so random, and all over the place.
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	I got some really cool footage but um, not like cute like Instagram girl footage, because I did dress like a 12 year old boy the whole time.
recommend	V;PRS;SG;1	recommend	So the first thing that I recommend you do in Portland is not actually in Portland at all.
be	V;NFIN	be	So you might be like this is a terrible list.
do	V;PST;PL;1	did	The number one thing that we did on our trip was we actually stayed a night in Eugene, Oregon, and we got up at like the crack of dawn and drove to Crater Lake.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	Um one of the coolest things that I have ever seen.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	The footage that I'm gonna put in here honestly it looks a little bit fake.
do	V;NFIN	do	We didn't do a whole lot of hiking here because there was not a whole lot of cloud coverage that day, and it was so hot.
do	V;PST;PL;1	did	So what we did was we drove around the entire perimeter of the lake and you get all these incredible views of the mountains and the lake, and seeing it from all the different angles was so worth it.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	So maybe this is not something that you want to do if you're just going to Portland for a couple of days but it's definitely something to consider if you're going out to Oregon for a little bit longer period of time.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	They're just sort of like what I thought of as I was making this video.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	So there's a lot of cool shopping in Portland, Oregon.
check	V;NFIN	check	And then one of the biggest things that you should check out while you're there is Powell's which is a bookstore.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	And I want to say it's like the the biggest like Indie Indie bookstore in America.
guess	V;PRS;SG;1	guess	I guess that's what it's called.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	I'm gonna – oh, it's the world's largest independent bookstore.
spend	V;PST;V.PTCP	spent	So this place was so cool we could have spent hours in here.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	The best thing that I thought about this bookstore was that they mixed in new copies of books with used copies.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	So if you're looking for a book and you're wondering like – oh I wonder if I could get this used, like it's usually right next to it, which I thought was just like so awesome.
love	V;PRS;SG;1	love	That was very exciting for me because I love a used book.
enjoy	V;NFIN	enjoy	Um I don't enjoy bugs.
go	V;NFIN	go	But um I will go on a good hike from now and again so just like you know where I'm coming from.
stop	V;NFIN	stop	My friend Andrea is a biology major so we did stop to look at many slugs that were on the ground and I did beeline it away from the slugs.
like	V;PRS;2;SG	like	But if you like slugs, you're in – you're in luck.
go	V;PST;PL;1	went	So the first couple days we were in Portland, we went hiking in Forest Park which you can get to by public transit in the city.
pop	V;PRS;PL;3	pop	It's really quick and some of the trails even pop you back out into like the city, so it's – it's really super accessible.
do	V;PST;PL;1	did	So we did the Wildwood Trail and we also did a tour of Pittock Mansion.
want	V;NFIN	want	Um if you don't want to do a tour of Pittock Mansion, I'd still recommend like taking the trail up there because it has a really cool like overlook of the city that you can go see.
love	V;PST;SG;1	loved	Um I loved doing that.
know	V;NFIN	know	I actually don't know if they're like technically in Forest Park but they're right in the same area.
imagine	V;NFIN	imagine	It's a lot of roses as you can imagine.
get	V;PRS;2;SG	get	You get to walk through all these gardens and different types of roses from different parts of the world and yeah it was just really cool.
have	V;NFIN	have	Then the Japanese gardens you do have to pay for but I will say that it was definitely worth it.
see	V;NFIN	see	If it's a clear day you can actually see Mount Hood from the Japanese gardens which was really wild, and the garden was just so pretty and quiet and serene.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were even some people like meditating in there which was kind of funny.
wear	V;NFIN	wear	I did wear my bird shirt – It's a pretty good bird shirt, I got a compliment on it – which I didn't say this earlier, but I bought this at the Audubon Society which I learned what that was.
know	V;NFIN	know	I didn't know what that was but it's um a society for um like bird watching and bird care and um they had a shirt that said bird nerd on it so I bought it because I thought it was funny.
approach	V;PST;V.PTCP	approached	But I was approached by – I want to say – at least three different people who asked me if I did bird watching like very seriously.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	So we are going to do a week of me vlogging.
bounce	V;PST;SG;1	bounced	I bounced around here and there, so I have to kind of show you guys around and show you what it's all about, and today I'm on general nuclear medicine, so let's start the day.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	So like I said, I'm on my general nuclear medicine rotation this week.
switch	V;PST;V.PTCP	switched	I get switched around -- moved around a few times to cover some residents who are on vacation, so I'll be at other spots later this week.
read	V;PST;V.PTCP	read	So so far this morning I've just read a few studies (it's a little slow in the morning) of general nuclear medicine because a lot of stuff takes time for the radio tracers to go into the body or the specific organ we are targeting, so we have to wait a little bit in the morning.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	So I've done three lymphoscintigraphies.
inject	V;PRS;PL;1	inject	For lymphoscintigraphies we basically inject technetium in skin surrounding a melanoma and after we inject we wait about 15-20 minutes, maybe up to an hour, and wait for that radio tracer to travel to the closest lymph node which we would call the sentinel lymph node.
do	V;PRS;PL;1	do	What we do is we mark it under our cameras and then the patient will go up to the OR, have the melanoma, a wide excision of the melanoma, and then they will try to find the lymph node that we marked and take that one out as well to make sure there's no metastasis.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	So I've done three of those this morning.
read	V;NFIN	read	I will read a few more scans coming up here shortly and just trying to tie up some loose ends before I go to conference.
check	V;NFIN	check	I'll check back in afterwards.
feel	V;PRS;SG;3	feels	Ah, the sun feels so good on the face.
get	V;PRS;PL;1	get	We get to head home early today because we finished off the scans, and I'm gonna go home and work out because I was too lazy to do it this morning because for some reason I was tired from having a weekend off, which makes no sense.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	But I'm gonna go work out, cook something for dinner and do this all over again tomorrow.
stay	V;2;NFIN	stay	We're on a different rotation tomorrow so stay tuned to find out which one.
pull	V;PST;V.PTCP	pulled	It is Tuesday and today I got pulled to ultrasound.
know	V;NFIN	know	Yes, I don't know why I got pulled on today, but I did, so probably just sit in there, read some ultrasounds and get out early.
need	V;PRS;SG;1	need	I need to film another YouTube video.
crush	V;PRS;V.PTCP	crushing	Alright so I've been here just crushing ultrasounds, and today I've been feeling a little under the weather so I've been, like, yeah, I've been like crushing these all day today and then I'll show you what else I've been.
pick	V;PST;SG;1	picked	I picked these up when I was in Amsterdam for ...
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	I got sick when I was on Croatia for vacation.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have no idea what it says, but I think they're cough drops, so if anybody knows what this says let me know.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	So this is what I have been doing all day is crushing ultrasounds and having Dutch cough drops.
speak	V;PRS;SG;3	speaks	So if anybody speaks Dutch tell me what that means.
try	V;PRS;SG;1	try	Oh and PS, I usually try to avoid taking medicines that I can't read the name of but I had someone help me at the pharmacy in the airport.
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	She spoke a little bit of English and I think those are actually cough drops, so, that's the only reason I'm still eating them today.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	You can tell by my voice it's a little on the sore side today so hopefully I get better because, I haven't told you guys yet, but I'm going somewhere this weekend and it's gonna be fun.
get	V;NFIN	get	Now it's time to get back to work.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	So I'm officially going home right now.
go	V;NFIN	go	Yeah, normally I would go home and work out, especially since it's, like, a beautiful day I would probably go running.
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	But since I feel kind of crappy today I'm just going to go home, hang out, rest (ambulance was a little loud sorry).
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gon	So I'm probably just gonna go home and rest and I have to get my hair cut today for my big trip coming up.
know	V;NFIN	know	But you still don't know where I'm going.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	But yeah that's pretty much it, hope you guys don't mind this kind of like random vlog that I'm doing, but I just wanted to show you guys kind of loosely like how a week is and the life of a radiology resident.
find	V;PRS;PL;3	find	So hopefully you guys find it somewhat interesting, and if you do I'll keep doing it.
head	V;PRS;V.PTCP	heading	So heading home now.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
construct	V;PST;V.PTCP	constructed	The first pre-historic settlements was constructed in 3000 BC around the hill of Acropolis.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The legend says that the King of Athens, Theseus unified the ten tribes of early Athens into one kingdom (c. 1230 BC).
bring	V;PRS;V.PTCP	bringing	This process of synoikismos – bringing together in one home – created the largest and wealthiest state on the Greek mainland, but it also created a larger class of people excluded from political life by the nobility.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	By the 7th century BC, social unrest had become widespread, and the Areopagus appointed Draco to draft a strict new law code (hence "draconian").
fail	V;PST;SG;3	failed	When this failed, they appointed Solon, with a mandate to create a new constitution (594).
experience	V;PST;SG;3	experienced	During the Middle Ages, Athens experienced a decline, but re-emerged under Byzantian rule.
thrive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thriving	Athens was thriving and prosperous during the Crusades, actually benefiting from the Italian trade during this period.
suffer	V;PST;SG;3	suffered	However, this fruitful period was short-lived, as Greece suffered badly under the Ottoman Empire, only to recover in the 19th century as the capital of independent Greece.
host	V;PST;SG;3	hosted	Athens hosted the 2004 Summer Olympic Games.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	While most of the sporting venues were located outside the city proper - in various locations throughout Attica - the entire urban area of Athens underwent major lasting changes that have improved the quality of life for visitors and residents alike.
complete	V;PST;V.PTCP	completed	Aside from the excellent transportation infrastructure that was completed in time for the 2004 Olympics (from new freeways to light rail systems), the city's historic center underwent serious renovation.
connect	V;PRS;SG;3	connects	Most notable among the city's facelift projects are the Unification of Archaeological Sites - which connects the city's classical - era ruins and monuments to each other through a network of pleasant pedestrianized streets - and the restoration of the picturesque neoclassical Thissio and Pláka districts.
take	V;PST;PL;3	took	The ancient Olympic Games took place in Olympia from 776 BCE to 394 AD.
visit	V;NFIN	visit	It is a lengthy day trip from Athens to visit Olympia, but quite interesting.
choose	V;PST;V.PTCP	chosen	Athens was just a small provincial village when it was chosen in the 1830s to serve as the national capital of the modern Greek State.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Although it had a prestigious past, the city's political, economic, and cultural importance had declined over the centuries, leaving behind only its classical ruins as a reminder of better times.
move	V;NFIN	move	With the decision to move the national capital from Nafplio to Athens, architects and city planners were hired to build a new city next to the classical ruins, with grand neoclassical homes and public buildings, large city squares, green spaces, and wide avenues, making a conscious, decisive turn from the city's Ottoman past.
regain	V;PST;SG;3	regained	The city regained its importance in Greek civilization, and by 1900 had evolved into a very attractive cosmopolitan city, with abundant neoclassical architecture harking to the nation's past.
mark	V;PST;SG;3	marked	The 20th century however, marked the rapid development of Athens.
suffer	V;PST;SG;3	suffered	The city suffered minor damage during WWII, and suffered extensive urban planning in the decades that followed, as the nation rapidly industrialized and urbanized.
demolish	V;PST;V.PTCP	demolished	In the 1960s and 1970s, many 19th century neoclassical buildings, often small and private, were demolished to make way for office buildings, often designed by great Greek architects.
expand	V;PST;SG;3	expanded	The city also expanded outward through rash development, particularly towards the west, as its population grew by absorbing job-seekers from the provinces.
reduce	V;PST;PL;3	reduced	With the onset of the automobile, public officials reduced the city's public transportation services without foreseeing the traffic gridlock and smog that would menace the city by the 1980s.
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	By the late 1980s and early 1990s, the city's reality led to a rude awakening among local and national officials and, coupled with the country's new found remarkable prosperity, large scale projects began to slowly regenerate the city and undo some of the damages of recent decades.
pour	V;PST;V.PTCP	poured	Over the course of the next 15 years, money was poured into new transportation infrastructure projects, the restoration of surviving neoclassical buildings, the gentrification of the city's historical center and the renovation of many former industrial areas and the city's coastline.
accompany	V;PST;V.PTCP	accompanied	The restoration of charming neoclassical buildings in the city's historical center has been accompanied by the construction of attractive post-modern buildings in newer districts; both of which have begun to improve the aesthetic essence of the city.
evolve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	evolving	Athens today is ever evolving, forging a brand new identity for the 21st century.
visit	V;NFIN	visit	Spring and late autumn are the best times to visit Athens.
happen	V;PRS;SG;3	happens	Summer can be extremely hot and dry during heatwaves, but this rarely happens.
save	V;NFIN	save	Winter is definitely low season, with the occasional rainy or snowy day, but also an ideal time to save money while enjoying the city without countless other travelers and tourists.
attribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	attributed	The main reason attributed for the pollution of Athens is because the city is enclosed by mountains in a basin which does not let the smog leave.
improve	V;NFIN	improve	The government's ban on diesel vehicles within Athens and the early 1990s initiatives to improve car emissions have greatly contributed to better atmospheric conditions in the basin.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It is the largest town on the island and has the largest share of accommodations.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	The area is famous for its World War II wreck diving, and the site has been named in many lists of top dive spots in the world.
hide	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hiding	In September 1944, a fleet of Japanese ships hiding in the harbor were sunk in a daring raid by the US navy.
preserve	V;PST;V.PTCP	preserved	The result is around ten well preserved underwater shipwrecks surrounded with coral reef.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are also attractions on Coron Island itself.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are many beautiful white sand beaches, mostly tiny and surrounded by large limestone cliffs and wildlife.
snorkel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	snorkeling	Barracuda and Kayangan lakes are both stunning locations, and good for snorkeling, and the island is the ancestral domain of an indigenous tribe who are managing the island in a sustainable way and keeping outsiders at a distance and offshore at night.
belong	V;PRS;PL;3	belong	The first inhabitants of Coron were the Tagbanuas who belong to the second wave of Indonesians who migrated to this area some 5,000 years ago.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	living	They were a nomadic, seafaring people, living mainly by fishing and subsistence agriculture.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	Although they are now sedentary (with the young using cell phones, etc.), they maintain many of their old customs, traditions and beliefs.
remain	V;PRS;PL;3	remain	Today, the Tagbanuas remain the dominant if not entire population of Coron.
register	V;PST;V.PTCP	registered	In 1902 that Coron was registered as a town and the name of the town was officially changed from Penon de Coron to Coron.
experience	V;PST;SG;3	experienced	From 1939 to the outbreak of World War II, the municipality experienced the mining boom.
shift	V;PST;SG;3	shifted	Labor shifted from farming to mining.
occupy	V;PST;PL;3	occupied	In July 1942 the Japanese occupied the mining camps and resumed operation of the manganese mines.
sink	V;PST;V.PTCP	sunk	On September 24, 1944, a group of Japanese ships were sunk by American warplanes in Coron waters as the ships retreated from Manila Bay.
comprise	V;PRS;PL;3	comprise	To this day, about 10 or 12 of these World War II Japanese shipwrecks comprise what is considered one of the best dive sites in the world.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	In 1947, large scale deep sea fishing was introduced to Coron, and the town experienced another boom, a fishing boom.
increase	V;PST;SG;3	increased	The population increased, as many people from Luzon and the Visayas came to work either as fishermen or miners.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	On June 17, 1950, Busuanga was officially created as a separate municipality from Coron and in 1954, Coron was further reduced by the official creation of the Municipality of Linapacan.
reduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	reduced	On September 12, 1992, Coron was finally reduced by the official creation of the Municipality of Culion.
remain	V;PRS;SG;3	remains	It remains a small, quaint fishing town with laid back charm but with increasing media exposure it is growing, slowly but steadily,
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	Coron has taken an important position in the tourism industry.
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	In the past decade, there has been a rapid influx of scuba divers and other tourists coming in, making tourism the major industry player in Coron today.
tell	V;NFIN	Tell	How to Tell a Joke
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Getting	Getting the Material Right
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Getting	Getting the Delivery Right
tell	V;PRS;2;SG	tell	From one-liners to classic three-liners to the one-minute gag you tell your friends, a good joke pleases everyone.
ease	V;NFIN	ease	Joke-telling is one of the best ways to ease tension, make a new friend, or light up a room.
get	V;NFIN	get	That is, of course, if you can get a laugh.
tell	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Telling	Telling good jokes is an art that comes naturally to some people, but for others it takes practice and hard work.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Getting	Getting the Material Right
know	V;IMP;2	Know	Know your audience.
tell	V;PRS;2;SG	tell	All aspects of the joke you tell, from the content to the length, need to be suited to your audience.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	What’s funny to a group of 20-year old college students may be very different from what makes your 70-year old-uncle laugh (then again, maybe not).
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Everyone’s an individual, so there are no hard and fast rules.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	But, unless you personally know the members of your audience, here are some good rules of thumb to follow:
like	V;NFIN	like	elderly people won’t like crude jokes;
stay	V;IMP;2	stay	stay away from misogynist jokes if you’ve got an audience full of women;
enjoy	V;NFIN	enjoy	people of a specific ethnic or racial group won’t enjoy jokes making fun of their group;
require	V;PRS;V.PTCP	requiring	and jokes requiring specialized knowledge (of, for example, science or old movies) will only be appreciated by people who possess that knowledge.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	The more you know about your audience the better you’ll be able to tailor your jokes to them.
choose	V;IMP;2	Choose	Choose great material.
find	V;NFIN	find	You can find fodder lots of places — from your life, joke files online, by repurposing jokes you’ve heard in the past, and so on.
want	V;NFIN	want	You may want to start your own joke file.
write	V;NFIN	write	You can write your jokes down on index cards to keep them handy or use a document file on your computer. [1]
allow	V;NFIN	allow	The latter option may allow for easier revision.
decide	V;IMP;2	Decide	Decide on a target.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Every joke has a “target,” which is what the joke is about.
suit	V;PST;V.PTCP	suited	It’s important that the joke’s target (the most basic element of your material) be suited to your audience.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure it’s a target your audience will be interested in and something they’re likely willing to laugh at. [2]
laugh	V;NFIN	laugh	For example, husbands are likely to laugh at jokes about wives and vice versa;
find	V;NFIN	find	students will typically find jokes about school and teachers humorous.
have	V;IMP;2	Have	Have a realistic but exaggerated setup.
have	V;NFIN	have	The opening of the joke — or setup — should have a basis in the real world so your audience can relate to it, but it should also include exaggeration because this is what gives a joke its humorous edge. [3]
think	V;IMP;2	Think	Think of the setup as the foundation of a story.
lay	V;NFIN	lay	if you don’t lay a good foundation here, then the punchline won’t make sense to the audience or they won’t find it humorous.
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	Your setup needs to be both realistic and exaggerated in order to be funny —
place	V;PRS;V.PTCP	placing	it’s placing these two incongruous elements side by side that makes the joke funny. [4]
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	it just depends on the individual joke.
surprise	V;IMP;2	Surprise	Surprise with the punchline.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	This is where the payoff comes in, what makes the joke succeed or fail.
want	V;PRS;2;SG	want	If you want to make the audience laugh, your punchline needs to be surprising.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Some jokes also have what is called a tag or topper, which is an additional punchline.
build	V;PRS;SG;3	builds	The tag builds on the original punchline or twists back on it in a surprising way.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make the joke your own.
rehash	V;PRS;PL;3	rehash	Lots of jokes rehash the same ground and sometimes they retell a story countless other jokes have told.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	For your joke to be funny, it has to surprise the audience in some way, which means it has to seem original or new.
personalize	V;NFIN	personalize	One way to personalize a joke is to change the ending. [5]
dress	V;NFIN	dress	Another option is to dress the joke up as a story about your own life.
make	V;NFIN	make	This will make a familiar joke unrecognizable.
make	V;NFIN	make	It may also make it particularly interesting if your audience is your friend(s). [6]
know	V;IMP;2	Know	Know your material.
practice	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Practicing	Practicing your joke is crucial.
need	V;NFIN	need	You don’t need to have it completely memorized — in fact, you "shouldn’t" memorize it — but you need to be really comfortable with it, so comfortable that you can continue on with telling it even if you get nervous or sidetracked, which is very possible once you’re in front of an audience.
memorize	V;PST;V.PTCP	Memorized	Memorized jokes sound wooden, like they are being read off a script instead of relayed by a friend or entertainer.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Good jokes have a lot of details and personality, so don’t be afraid to embellish. [7]
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try different things out and see what feels right, what sounds best.
use	V;IMP;2	use	Don’t use a joke until you’re completely comfortable with it.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try recording your joke on a tape player and playing it back to yourself.
hear	V;PRS;2;SG	hear	If you hear a lot of awkward pauses or “ah”s or “um”s, your joke isn’t ready and you need to practice more. [8]
try	V;NFIN	try	You can also try practicing in front of a sympathetic friend or family member once you’re ready for a “real” audience.
wash	V;NFIN	Wash	How to Wash Overalls in a Washing Machine
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	Whether you are a working man or just a little cutie trying to stay cute, those overalls are going to need washing sooner or later.
twist	V;PST;V.PTCP	twisted	The straps on adults’ overalls often get so twisted during the wash that one wishes she had just used an old fashioned washboard instead.
wear	V;NFIN	wear	Your appliances are also murder on the straps, which are almost always the first things to wear out on your overalls.
show	V;NFIN	show	This article will show you how to save time and prevent wear, extending the life of your overalls until they are so soft you could wear them to bed.
wash	V;IMP;2	Wash	Wash alone or with "like" clothing.
wash	V;NFIN	wash	It’s best to wash adults’ overalls alone, especially men’s.
wash	V;NFIN	wash	However, it is okay to wash just a few items with them, like blue jeans.
want	V;NFIN	want	What you don’t want to wash with them is anything that can get twisted up with the straps, like women’s long sleeve shirts or kids’ blankets.
think	V;IMP;2	Think	Think similar clothing, minus the twisty-ness.
do	V;IMP;2	do	Just do the denim and canvas stuff together.
fasten	V;IMP;2	Fasten	Fasten the straps to the bib.
know	V;NFIN	know	If you didn’t know already, the bib is the top front part with the breast pockets.
go	V;IMP;2	Go	Go ahead and fasten both straps before you wash.
help	V;NFIN	help	This will help keep them from twisting up during the wash.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use twist ties to keep the straps fastened.
reapportion	V;NFIN	reapportion	This step will reapportion years of your life in the long run.
skip	V;IMP;2	skip	Don’t skip it.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take the 20 seconds to use a couple twist ties on the fasteners to make sure they don’t come loose.
wash	V;PRS;V.PTCP	washing	If your washing machine is worth its salt, it will undo those straps in a heartbeat and tie them in a sheet bend unless you prevent it.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	All it takes is two twist ties and a few seconds of patience.
empty	V;IMP;2	Empty	Empty the pockets.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Overalls have more pockets than pants.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The bib has a wallet pocket and a watch pocket.
put	V;PRS;PL;3	put	Kids put rocks, candy, and bugs in these.
put	V;PRS;PL;3	put	Men put cell phones and small tools in them.
defy	V;PRS;PL;3	defy	Women defy the laws of purse-space, so when it comes to bib pockets, the sky is the limit.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure all the pockets are empty.
move	V;IMP;2	Move	Move the strap adjusters around.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	You’ve probably got them just the way you like them, but if you move the adjusters up or down on the straps occasionally, the straps will last longer.
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Depending	Depending on the adjusters, you may be able to leave them a little loose too.
wear	V;PRS;PL;3	wear	The straps wear out fastest around the adjusters over the course of numerous wash and dry cycles (not to mention being out in the elements if they are work clothes).
move	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Moving	Moving them distributes the wear and tear of washing.
rotate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	rotating	This is the same idea as rotating tires.
know	V;PRS;2;SG	know	You know about rotating your tires, don't you?
wash	V;PRS;2;SG	wash	If you wash your overalls alone or in a light load, use about half the detergent called for and less water.
leave	V;IMP;2	Leave	Leave the straps fastened and twist-tied to the bib when you throw them in the dryer.
cut	V;NFIN	cut	This will cut down on some of the racket, but you're still going to hear them.
deteriorate	V;NFIN	deteriorate	The paper-type twist ties will deteriorate a little, but the dryer filter screen will catch the fallout.
hold	V;NFIN	hold	Plastic-type twist ties will hold up in the wash, but may or may not melt in your dryer.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use with caution.
